    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nCompanies overwhelmingly provided timely responses, providing a timely response to 99.6% of the more\nthan 5.9 million complaints sent to them for review in 2025.\n24\n24 Complaints in which a company did not provide a response within 15 calendar days of the complaint being sent to\nthe company—or within 60 days if it requested more time—are reflected in the Consumer Complaint Database as\nnot having received a timely response.15 BUREAU OF CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION\nTABLE 1: HOW COMPANIES HAVE RESPONDED TO CONSUMER COMPLAINTS\nFinancial product\nor service Closed with\nmonetary\nrelief\nClosed with\nnon-monetary\nrelief\nClosed with\nexplanation\nAdmin\nresponse\nCompany\nreviewing\nCompany\ndid not\nprovide a\ntimely\nresponse\nAll <1% 37% 53% 9% <1% <1%\nCredit or consumer\nreporting\n<1% 40% 51% 10% <1% <1%\nDebt collection <1% 23% 66% 7% <1% 3%\nCredit card 12% 19% 63% 5% <1% <1%\nChecking or savings 10% 5% 83% 1% <1% <1%\nMoney transfer or\nservice, virtual\ncurrency\n2% 2% 94% <1% <1% 1%\nMortgage 2% 3% 91% 3% <1% 1%\nVehicle loan or lease 2% 6% 88% 2% <1% 2%\nStudent loan <1% 1% 66% <1% 1% 31%\n25\nPersonal loan 3% 4% 86% 2% <1% 4%\nPrepaid card 14% 7% 71% 3% <1% 5%\nDebt or credit\nmanagement\n3% 10% 76% 3% <1% 8%\nPayday loan 2% 2% 86% 5% <1% 5%\nTitle loan 2% 6% 88% <1% <1% 3%\nDeposit advance 3% 2% 84% 3% <1% 8%\n25 See discussion infra Section 4.8.16 BUREAU OF CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION\n4. Complaint types\nThis section summarizes the types of complaints and issues identified by consumers in 2025.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\nThe CFPB received approximately 23,700 student loan complaints in 2025. It sent 21,500 (91%) of these\ncomplaints to companies for review and response, referred 6% to other regulatory agencies, and found 2%\nto be not actionable. As of March 2, 2026, 0.03% of student loan complaints were pending with the\nconsumer and 0.02% were pending with the CFPB.\nCompanies responded to 69% of student loan complaints sent to them for review and response. In 77% of\nthese complaints, consumers reported first attempting to resolve their issue with the company. Companies\nclosed 66% of them with an explanation, 0.2% with monetary relief, and 1% with non-monetary relief.\nThey provided an administrative response for 0.4% of complaints. Companies did not provide a timely\nresponse—or a response at all—for 31% of complaints.\n33 As of March 2, 2026, 1% of complaints were\npending review by the company.\nWhen submitting student loan complaints, consumers specify whether they are complaining about a\nfederal student loan or a private student loan. In 2025, consumers complained about federal student loans\nmore frequently than private student loans (see Figure 35).34\nFIGURE 35: STUDENT LOAN COMPLAINTS BY TYPE OF LOAN AND OUTCOMES\nConsumers also identify the issue that best describes the problem they experienced. The most common\nissue was Dealing with your lender or servicer (Figure 36).\n33 In 2025, companies reported that pending litigation over federal student loan repayment programs, as well as\nchanges in student loan servicing, resulted in untimely responses or no responses at all. See generally, Consumer\nFin. Prot. Bureau, Annual Report of the CFPB Private Education Student Loan Ombudsman (Jan. 2026),\nhttps://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/documents/cfpb_pelo-annual-report_2026-01.pdf (discussing student loan\ncomplaints and untimely responses).\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an intake evidence gap register for the student-loan evidence segment of the CFPB Consumer Response Annual Report 2025. Use only the two supplied report segments: the company-response segment containing the 99.6% company-wide timely-response rate, the 15-calendar-day and 60-calendar-day timeliness definition, and Table 1 response-category distribution by product, and the student-loan segment containing the approximately 23,700 complaints received, 21,500 sent to companies, 69% response rate, 66% closed with explanation, 31% no timely response or no response at all, and the as-of-March-2-2026 pending figures. Structure the register with the following level-2 headings in this order: `Scope and Source Materials`, `Timeliness Evidence`, `Gap Classification`, `Quantitative Comparison`, `Open Evidence`, and `Readiness Close`.\n\nPreserve every source figure, percentage, calendar-day term, product label, and as-of date exactly as supplied, and do not round, relabel, or convert any source value before completing a comparison or classification. In `Timeliness Evidence`, state the applicable 15-calendar-day response window and the 60-calendar-day extension condition, then determine which supplied student-loan figures are tied to the timeliness window and which are point-in-time pending counts that cannot be converted into timeliness results. In `Gap Classification`, identify the 31% of student-loan complaints for which companies did not provide a timely response or any response as a confirmed response gap, and separately flag the 1% pending company review as an unresolved gap whose final classification cannot be determined from the supplied materials.\n\nFor every gap-register entry, cite the exact source segment and figure or rule used, including the 99.6% company-wide timely-response rate and Table 1 row from the company-response segment and the 23,700 received, 21,500 sent, 69% responded, 66% explanation, 31% no-timely-response, and as-of-March-2-2026 pending figures from the student-loan segment. Classify each gap-register entry using only the labels `Confirmed Gap`, `Unresolved Gap`, or `Not Determinable`, and classify the student-loan segment against the company-wide response categories in Table 1 by assigning the category labels `Closed with explanation`, `Closed with monetary relief`, `Closed with non-monetary relief`, `Admin response`, `Company reviewing`, or `Company did not provide a timely response` where the supplied figures permit.\n\nIn `Quantitative Comparison`, compare the student-loan segment against the company-wide benchmark on the same timely-response basis by placing the 69% student-loan response rate and the 99.6% company-wide timely-response rate side by side, and compare the student-loan closure distribution against the Table 1 `All` row and the `Student loan` row, showing each comparison as an absolute percentage-point difference without converting any figure to a count. Use only the two supplied report segments and label any gap, comparison, or classification that cannot be established from those segments as `Not Determinable` rather than estimating or importing outside data. In `Open Evidence`, list every supplied figure or status that prevents a complete gap determination, including the 1% pending company review, the 0.03% pending with the consumer, and the 0.02% pending with the CFPB, and state which classification each one blocks.\n\nUse only the labels `Confirmed Gap`, `Unresolved Gap`, and `Not Determinable` for gap status, and do not use `closed`, `resolved`, or `reconciled` for any entry whose pending or no-response status remains open. State that the litigation-and-servicing explanation for untimely or absent student-loan responses is a company-reported reason contained in the annual report, and identify the cited Private Education Student Loan Ombudsman report as a referenced source rather than treating the explanation as an independently verified cause. Close the register with a one-sentence readiness gate that names the highest-priority unresolved evidence item from the student-loan segment and states that the gap register cannot be finalized until that item is resolved or explicitly marked `Not Determinable`.",
