    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nFIGURE 9: MONTHLY COMPLAINT VOLUME FOR TYPES OF CREDIT OR CONSUMER REPORTING\nCOMPLAINTS\nIn 2025, the monthly average for the top issue, Incorrect information on your report, increased 249%\ncompared to the monthly average for the prior two years (Figure 10).\nFIGURE 10: MONTHLY COMPLAINT VOLUME FOR ISSUES OF CREDIT OR CONSUMER REPORTING\nCOMPLAINTS20 BUREAU OF CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION\nThe most common complaint was about inaccurate or incomplete information. Consumers reported\nproblems with tradeline information (e.g., account and payment statuses, outstanding balances), public\nrecord information (e.g., bankruptcies and legal records), personal information (e.g., addresses, phone\nnumbers), inquiry information (e.g., hard and soft inquires), and collections (e.g., third-party collections).\nConsumers reported they were unable to obtain jobs and apartments due to errors on their credit reports.\nIn many of these complaints, consumers assert that the inaccurate information stems from identity theft or\nfraud. In support of these claims, they cite to or attach police reports or FTC identity theft reports.\nIn response to complaints about inaccurate or incomplete information, NCRAs generally treated these\ncomplaints as a dispute or a block of information request, and they responded by detailing how\ninformation was verified, updated, removed, or otherwise not being reported. Other CRAs and furnishers\ntook similar actions or summarized the results of the prior reinvestigation or block request. Some CRAs\nand furnishers reported that although they attempted to contact the consumer, those attempts were\nunsuccessful.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a population scope brief for the CFPB consumer response annual report 2025 evidence segment on credit or consumer reporting complaints. Use only the supplied segment text; if any population attribute cannot be established from that text, label it `Not Determinable`.\n\nIn the brief, use the exact issue label `Incorrect information on your report` and the exact comparison phrase `monthly average for the prior two years` wherever those concepts appear, and use `Not Determinable` rather than `unknown` for any missing population attribute. Preserve the source figure `249%`, the comparison period `2025`, and the baseline wording `prior two years` exactly, without rounding or paraphrasing, when describing the complaint-volume change.\n\nWrite for a CFPB annual report evidence reviewer, distinguishing reported source facts from consumer assertions and from reviewer inference in every section.\n\nIdentify the records consumers cited or attached, namely police reports and FTC identity theft reports, as the supporting documentation for the identity theft or fraud assertion, and state that no other supporting records are supplied in this segment.\n\nMap every population characterization in the brief back to the specific source passage, including the `249%` increase, the `prior two years` baseline, the listed tradeline, public record, personal information, inquiry, and collections categories, and the jobs and apartments impact.\n\nClassify each consumer-reported impact statement in the segment as `Source Fact`, `Consumer Assertion`, or `Reviewer Inference`, using the supplied text to determine whether the statement is a reported fact, a consumer claim, or an unsupported inference.\n\nCover the full set of issue subcategories named in the segment: tradeline information, public record information, personal information, inquiry information, and collections, and separately cover the identity theft or fraud assertion and the jobs and apartments impact.\n\nSeparate the report's observed aggregate facts, such as the complaint-volume increase and the listed issue categories, from consumer assertions about identity theft or fraud and from any reviewer inference about causation, and label each accordingly.",
