    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nFIGURE 9: MONTHLY COMPLAINT VOLUME FOR TYPES OF CREDIT OR CONSUMER REPORTING\nCOMPLAINTS\nIn 2025, the monthly average for the top issue, Incorrect information on your report, increased 249%\ncompared to the monthly average for the prior two years (Figure 10).\nFIGURE 10: MONTHLY COMPLAINT VOLUME FOR ISSUES OF CREDIT OR CONSUMER REPORTING\nCOMPLAINTS20 BUREAU OF CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION\nThe most common complaint was about inaccurate or incomplete information. Consumers reported\nproblems with tradeline information (e.g., account and payment statuses, outstanding balances), public\nrecord information (e.g., bankruptcies and legal records), personal information (e.g., addresses, phone\nnumbers), inquiry information (e.g., hard and soft inquires), and collections (e.g., third-party collections).\nConsumers reported they were unable to obtain jobs and apartments due to errors on their credit reports.\nIn many of these complaints, consumers assert that the inaccurate information stems from identity theft or\nfraud. In support of these claims, they cite to or attach police reports or FTC identity theft reports.\nIn response to complaints about inaccurate or incomplete information, NCRAs generally treated these\ncomplaints as a dispute or a block of information request, and they responded by detailing how\ninformation was verified, updated, removed, or otherwise not being reported. Other CRAs and furnishers\ntook similar actions or summarized the results of the prior reinvestigation or block request. Some CRAs\nand furnishers reported that although they attempted to contact the consumer, those attempts were\nunsuccessful.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a credit reporting complaints complaint issue classification brief for intake and profiling review using only the supplied CFPB annual report text. Begin by identifying the complaint issue categories the report describes for credit or consumer reporting complaints, including the top issue labeled Incorrect information on your report and the subcategories of tradeline information, public record information, personal information, inquiry information, and collections. For each issue category, classify it as either Incorrect information on your report or another named issue using only the report's own terminology, and classify each supporting-record type mentioned in the report as Police report, FTC identity theft report, or Unspecified supporting record. Separate report-stated facts about complaint volume and issue categories from consumer assertions about identity theft or fraud and from any reviewer inference about the cause of reported errors. Preserve the exact source figure of a 249% increase in the monthly average for the top issue and the exact comparison period of the prior two years wherever those elements are cited. For each issue category, state the harm consumers reported, including inability to obtain jobs and apartments, and distinguish that reported harm from any qualitative consequence that cannot be measured from the report. Identify the supporting records consumers cite or attach for identity theft or fraud claims, and list any documentation the report indicates is used to support inaccurate-information complaints without inventing additional record types. Present the classification results in a table with columns for Issue category, Report-stated basis, Consumer assertion, Supporting record, and Classification, and keep any narrative explanation outside the table. Map each classified issue category and each supporting-record type to the exact report passage that supplies it, keeping the source label beside every classification entry rather than providing only an end citation. Write the brief in a neutral, intake-analyst tone for a complaint triage reviewer, defining terms such as tradeline and NCRA on first use and avoiding any recommendation about how a specific consumer complaint should be resolved. Mark any classification element not supported by the supplied text as Not Determinable.",
