    "work_product": "payment scam exposure financial education intake planning brief — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nFraud and scams present an ever-increasing problem for consumers, and no population is\nimmune. They affect young and older people, servicemembers, veterans, and their families.\nYoung adults are more frequent victims, while older adults are more heavily impacted financially\nwhen they are victims. Although the FTC4 and the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI)5\n4 See, e.g., Fed. Trade Comm’n., Consumer Advice, Top Scams of 2024, (Mar. 10, 2025),\nhttps://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2025/03/top-scams-2024.\n5 See, e.g., Fed. Bureau of Investigation, Internet Crime Report (2024),\nhttps://www.ic3.gov/AnnualReport/Reports/2024_IC3Report.pdf.\n11 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\nreceived fraud and scam reports totaling $12.5 to $16.6 billion in 2024, the true cost could be six\nor seven times higher, because most fraud and scams6 go unreported to federal agencies.\nConsumers are increasingly describing issues related to fraud and scams in their complaints to\nthe CFPB. For example, as peer-to-peer (P2P) payment platforms have flourished in recent years,\nso have fraud and scams. Between January 1, 2018, and December 31, 2023, the CFPB saw a\nsignificant increase in the number of complaints about P2P services; a machine learning model\nestimated that about 38% of the complaints were about fraud, amounting to millions of dollars\nstolen from consumers. Complaints include impersonation scams like “me to me” scams as well\nas unauthorized transfers that involve account takeover and identity theft. Consumers also\ncomplained about P2P companies’ responses to fraud and scams, including unresponsive\ncustomer service.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\nAmerica, (Mar. 11, 2026), https://consumerfed.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/The-Scam-Economy_The-True-Cost-of-Online-Scams.pdf. This report conservatively estimates the true cost of scams in America is at least $119\nbillion annually using an analysis of underreporting patterns documented by the Bureau of Justice Statistics (BJS)\nand other researchers, the FBI ITC 2024 data, and third-party research.\n7 See, e.g., FINRA Inv. Educ. Found., Can Educational Interventions Reduce Susceptibility to Financial Fraud?,\n(March 2021). https://www.finrafoundation.org/sites/finrafoundation/files/2024-10/can-educational-interventions-reduce-susceptibility-to-financial-fraud_0_0.pdf\n12 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\nFraud and scams among young adults\nYoung adults, ages 20 to 29, are losing money to fraud and scams more often than people ages\n70 to 79.8 But young adults do not lose as much money, mainly because they do not have as\nmuch to lose. Despite being comfortable online, young people are more likely to be victims\nbecause they engage in riskier behaviors. Their lack of experience with personal finance makes\nthem more susceptible to fraudsters.9\nYoung people are more likely to lose money when shopping online via a payment app or service\nafter being contacted through social media.10 A survey of young people ages 16 to 34 found that\nthey are very likely to share personal information online. Two-fifths of young people share their\nfinancial and payment details online (42% of 16- to 24-year-olds and 46% of 25- to 34-year-olds). This opens them up to fraud and scams, including identity theft and fraudulent investment\noffers.\n\nQUERY:\nProduce a payment scam exposure financial education intake planning brief using only the supplied CFPB financial literacy annual report excerpts. The brief must support intake planning for financial education by identifying which populations face the greatest payment scam exposure, which scam types are most relevant, and where the evidence is too incomplete to support a firm planning conclusion.\n\nFirst, calculate the duration of the P2P complaint observation window from January 1, 2018 through December 31, 2023 and state the applicable period in the brief. Then compare the financial impact and frequency of payment scam victimization between young adults and older adults using the same metric and period, and rank the two age groups by severity of financial impact.\n\nClassify each complaint type mentioned in the P2P complaint evidence as one of `Impersonation scam`, `Unauthorized transfer`, `Account takeover`, `Identity theft`, or `Other reported issue`, using only the categories supported by the supplied text.\n\nUse the exact label `Open Evidence` as the heading for the section listing missing or unknown inputs, and use the exact label `Next action:` once to introduce the single follow-up step required by the brief.\n\nIdentify the underreporting limitation stated in the CFPB report as missing evidence and list it under `Open Evidence`, stating which exposure calculation or planning conclusion it prevents.\n\nMap every numeric input, classification, comparison, and risk statement in the brief to the specific report excerpt and figure or rule it comes from, keeping source labels beside each item rather than only at the end.\n\nFlag the reported deficiency in P2P company responses to fraud and scams, specifically unresponsive customer service, as a confirmed gap in the supplied evidence and state which intake planning risk it creates.\n\nCover all of the following issue types in the brief: impersonation scams, unauthorized transfers, account takeover, identity theft, and unresponsive P2P company customer service.\n\nReturn exactly three numbered findings in the brief, ordered from largest to smallest financial impact, and show every percentage with exactly two decimal places.\n\nFor each finding, state the affected population, the plausible financial exposure or consequence, and the evidence limitation that prevents a fully quantified impact estimate.",
