    "work_product": "credit availability lifecycle credit availability research scoping brief — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nTickets, Presales & More, https://www.americanexpress.com/en-us/benefits/entertainment/amex-presale/(accessed\nOct. 16, 2025).\n184 See, e.g., Alaska Airlines, Alaska Airlines and Bank of America present a new premium credit card designed for\nglobal travelers, the Atmos™ Rewards Summit Visa Infinite® card, (Aug. 20, 2025),\nhttps://news.alaskaair.com/loyalty/alaska-airlines-and-bank-of-america-present-a-new-premium-credit-card/.\n185 For more on credit card purchase volume, see Sections 2.3 and 2.4. For more on rewards partnerships, see CFPB,\nCredit Card Rewards Issue Spotlight (May 2024), https://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/documents/cfpb_credit-card-rewards_issue-spotlight_2024-05.pdf, and 2023 Report, at 98–106.\n186 See, e.g., “As you look at new account growth, we're not originating anything less than 660.” Wells Fargo &\nCompany, Q2 2024 Earnings Call Transcript (July 12, 2024), https://www.capitaliq.spglobal.com/apisv3/spg-webplatform-core/docviewer?mid=226704260.\n187 See, e.g. “Across our cards portfolio, approximately 85% of our loans are to consumers with FICO scores of 660 or\nhigher.” Citigroup Inc., Q3 2024 Earnings Call Transcript (Oct. 15, 2025),\nhttps://www.capitaliq.spglobal.com/apisv3/spg-webplatform-core/docviewer?mid=231239297. 107 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\n6. Availability of credit\nAs in prior reports, in this section we examine measures relating to credit card availability. We\nexplore two broad areas: new account origination and credit availability after origination. To do\nso, we examine the credit card account life cycle, starting with marketing and consumer\napplications across a range of channels. Next, we track issuer approvals as well as new account\nand line origination, with additional information regarding the impact of new account fraud.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\nConsumers in all credit score tiers spend higher amounts in the first few months after opening\naccounts with promotional interest rates than accounts without these promotions. Comparing\nboth panels in Figure 32, which show purchase volumes on cards with and without introductory\npromotional interest rates in their first 36 months after origination. We observe higher spending\nlevels by cardholders with promotional accounts in the first three months than cardholders\nwithout promotional accounts during this time. However, average purchase volumes for both\ntypes of cards are very similar to each other three years after account opening.94\n94 We note that introductory promotional interest rates are just one factor that could be affecting different spending\nhabits in the first months after origination in these two groups. For example, there may be differences in the share of\naccounts that pursue rewards-based welcome bonuses—61.5 percent of credit card offers with introductory\npromotional interest rates come with rewards-based welcome bonuses, compared to 20 percent of credit card offers\nwithout introductory promotional interest rates, based on data from Competiscan.\n35%\n60%\n55%\n40%\n0%\n10%\n20%\n30%\n40%\n50%\n60%\n70%\nBelow-prime Prime Prime plus Superprime56 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\nFIGURE 32\nAverage purchase volume by month since origination on\ngeneral purpose cards by origination credit score (Y-14)\nPANEL A: WITH INTRODUCTORY APR PROMOTIONS\nPANEL B: WITHOUT INTRODUCTORY APR PROMOTIONS\nConsumers with promotional interest rate cards tend to carry relatively high average balances,95\nwith balances highest during the months typically associated with promotional periods. Recall\n95 See Section 4.1.2 for more on revolving balances.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a credit availability lifecycle credit availability research scoping brief using only the two supplied CFPB report sections. The brief must be neutral and reviewer-facing, distinguishing what the CFPB report states, what issuer representatives stated on earnings calls, and what remains undetermined. State explicitly that the supplied material is a CFPB report section describing its review approach and that the Wells Fargo and Citigroup quotations are illustrative earnings-call statements cited by the report, not independent regulatory findings or current obligations. Cover the two named review areas from the CFPB availability section—new account origination and credit availability after origination—as distinct topics, and address the marketing, application, approval, and new-account fraud elements the section identifies as part of the account life cycle. Present the brief as a table with columns Evidence Source, Covered Topic, Source Status, and Analytical Use, followed by a short narrative section for the promotional-APR comparison and threshold observations. Order the table rows by the two review areas named in the CFPB section before any supplementary observations. Keep the full brief between 260 and 340 words. For the promotional-APR comparison, compare average purchase volume in the first three months after origination for cards with introductory APR promotions against cards without such promotions, using the same origination credit score tiers and first-36-month measurement window from Figure 32, and rank the two promotional status groups by spending level. For the threshold observations, apply the 660 FICO origination cutoff quoted from Wells Fargo and the 85% of loans at 660 or higher figure quoted from Citigroup as threshold reference points, preserving the exact numbers, comparison operators, and portfolio scope stated in each quotation, and state whether each quotation provides enough population detail to support a threshold test or must be marked Not Determinable. Map every covered topic, comparison, and threshold observation to the specific CFPB section, quotation, or figure from which it is drawn, and place the source label beside each input rather than only at the end. Preserve the exact figures, dates, entity names, and quoted language from the supplied sections, including the 660 threshold, the approximately 85% portfolio figure, the 61.5% and 20% welcome-bonus shares, and the October 2025 access date, without rounding or paraphrasing any controlling number. Show every percentage exactly as it appears in the source. Mark any requested conclusion that cannot be established from the supplied sections as Not Determinable.",
