    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: § 240.17a-4 Records to be preserved by certain exchange members, brokers and dealers.]\n\n(13) The written policies and procedures the broker-dealer establishes, documents, maintains, and enforces to assess creditworthiness for the purpose of § 240.15c3-1(c)(2)(vi)(E), (c)(2)(vi)(F)( 1 ) and ( 2 ), and (c)(2)(vi)(H) or § 240.18a-1(c)(1)(vi)( 2 ), as applicable.\n\n(14) A copy of information required to be reported under §§ 242.901 through 242.909 of this chapter (Regulation SBSR).\n\n(15) Copies of documents, communications, disclosures, and notices related to business conduct standards as required under §§ 240.15Fh-1 through 240.15Fh-6 and 240.15Fk-1.\n\n(16) Copies of documents used to make a reasonable determination with respect to special entities, including information relating to the financial status, the tax status, the investment or financing objectives of the special entity as required under section 15F(h)(4)(C) and (5)(A) of the Act (15 U.S.C. 78 o -10(h)(4)(C) and (5)(A)).\n\n(17) The written probability of default determination, relied upon by such broker or dealer, pursuant to § 242.101(c)(2)(i) or § 242.102(d)(2)(i) of this chapter (Rule 101 or Rule 102 of Regulation M), as applicable.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: § 240.17a-4 Records to be preserved by certain exchange members, brokers and dealers.]\n\n(9) The records required to be made pursuant to § 240.15c3-3(d)(5) and (o) or § 240.18a-4, as applicable.\n\n(10) The records required to be made pursuant to § 240.15c3-4 and the results of the periodic reviews conducted pursuant to § 240.15c3-4(d).\n\n(11) All notices relating to an internal broker-dealer system provided to the customers of the broker or dealer that sponsors such internal broker-dealer system, as defined in paragraph (a)(16)(ii)(A) of § 240.17a-3. Notices, whether written or communicated through the internal broker-dealer trading system or other automated means, must be preserved under this paragraph (b)(11) if they are provided to all customers with access to an internal broker-dealer system, or to one or more classes of customers. Examples of notices to be preserved under this paragraph (b)(11) include, but are not limited to, notices addressing hours of system operations, system malfunctions, changes to system procedures, maintenance of hardware and software, and instructions pertaining to access to the internal broker-dealer system.\n\n(12) The records required to be made pursuant to § 240.15c3-1e(c)(4)(vi) or § 240.18a-1(e)(2)(iii)(F)( 2 ), as applicable.\n\nMATERIAL 3:\n[Continues under: § 240.17a-4 Records to be preserved by certain exchange members, brokers and dealers.]\n\n(1) All records required under § 240.17a-3(a)(12) until at least three years after the associated person's employment and any other connection with the member, broker or dealer has terminated.\n\n(2) All records required under § 240.17a-3(a)(13) until at least three years after the termination of employment or association of those persons required by § 240.17f-2 to be fingerprinted.\n\n(3) All records required pursuant to § 240.17a-3(a)(15) during the life of the enterprise.\n\n(4) All records required pursuant to § 240.17a-3(a)(14) for three years.\n\n(5) All account record information required pursuant to § 240.17a-3(a)(17) and all records required pursuant to § 240.17a-3(a)(35), in each case until at least six years after the earlier of the date the account was closed or the date on which the information was collected, provided, replaced, or updated.\n\n(6) Each report which a securities regulatory authority or the Commodity Futures Trading Commission has requested or required the member, broker or dealer to make and furnish to it pursuant to an order or settlement, and each securities regulatory authority, Commodity Futures Trading Commission, or prudential regulator examination report until three years after the date of the report.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an operational decision evidence sufficiency brief for the § 240.17a-4 records evidence segment, using only the supplied text from § 240.17a-4 and its cross-references. Begin with a one-sentence statement of the overall evidence sufficiency posture for the segment, then organize the brief under the level-2 headings `Evidence`, `Retention Assessment`, `Sufficiency Determination`, and `Open Items` in that order.\n\nUnder `Evidence`, list each covered record category by its paragraph number in the supplied § 240.17a-4 text and identify the cross-referenced rule or statutory provision that generates the record. Cover as distinct topics the creditworthiness policies and procedures under paragraph (13); Regulation SBSR reporting information under paragraph (14); business conduct standards documents under paragraph (15); special entity determination documents under paragraph (16); probability of default determinations under paragraph (17); the § 240.15c3-3(d)(5) and (o) or § 240.18a-4 records under paragraph (9); the § 240.15c3-4 records and periodic review results under paragraph (10); internal broker-dealer system notices under paragraph (11); and the § 240.15c3-1e(c)(4)(vi) or § 240.18a-1(e)(2)(iii)(F)(2) records under paragraph (12). Keep the paragraph number and cross-referenced citation beside every record category and every retention period, and state explicitly that the supplied materials are current rule text under § 240.17a-4, not staff guidance or a proposal.\n\nUnder `Retention Assessment`, for each record category with a specified retention period, state the retention start trigger, the required duration, and the retention end date rule using the exact periods supplied in the text, including \"until at least three years after the associated person's employment and any other connection with the member, broker or dealer has terminated\", \"until at least three years after the termination of employment or association of those persons required by § 240.17f-2 to be fingerprinted\", \"during the life of the enterprise\", \"for three years\", \"until at least six years after the earlier of the date the account was closed or the date on which the information was collected, provided, replaced, or updated\", and \"until three years after the date of the report\". Preserve the exact paragraph numbers, cross-referenced rule citations, statutory references, and retention phrases wherever they control a record category or retention period.\n\nUnder `Sufficiency Determination`, for each record category, connect the specific § 240.17a-4 paragraph as the governing requirement, the cross-referenced rule as the record source, and the resulting sufficiency status in one traceable chain. Use only the labels `Sufficient`, `Insufficient`, and `Not Determinable` for the evidence sufficiency status of each record category, and do not use `approved`, `closed`, or `reconciled` anywhere in the brief. If the supplied text does not specify a retention period for a covered record category, assign the retention status `Not Determinable` and identify the missing retention trigger or duration needed to complete the assessment. Do not add external facts, customary practice, or remembered requirements, and label any category whose sufficiency cannot be established from the supplied text as `Not Determinable`.\n\nUnder `Open Items`, list the records and documents required to be preserved under each covered paragraph and identify the cross-referenced source rule that generates each record, without inventing documents beyond those named in the supplied text. End the brief with a `Next action:` line tied to the highest-priority unresolved evidence gap.",
