    "work_product": "decision evidence sufficiency brief for cfpb financial literacy annual report 2025 evidence segment ctx010 — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n15 See e.g., Fed. Trade Comm’n, Protecting Older Consumers 2024-2025, (Dec. 1, 2025).\nhttps://www.ftc.gov/system/files/ftc_gov/pdf/P144400-OlderAdultsReportDec2025.pdf.\n16 See e.g., Fed. Trade Comm’n, Consumer Sentinel Network Data Book, (March (2025).\nhttps://www.ftc.gov/system/files/ftc_gov/pdf/csn-annual-data-book-2024.pdf.\n14 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\nAs mentioned in previous sections, the CFPB also offered training and informational webinars\nrelated to fraud and scams to provide financial education to specific populations, including older\nadults and servicemembers.\n15 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\n3. Optimizing and measuring\nfinancial education and financial\nwell-being\nFinEd develops and maintains a broad array of digital financial education resources, from youth\nfinancial education materials to the popular set of questions and answers, Ask CFPB. FinEd also\nengages with the research community to measure the effectiveness of financial education\nprograms and methods.\nTo understand the effectiveness of its financial education efforts, FinEd uses two types of\nevaluation: process and impact. Process metrics gauge the format and delivery of financial\neducation content. Impact metrics examine changes in outcomes, such as increases in financial\nwell-being scores or decreases in debt. Through both types of metrics, the CFPB learns about\nwhat makes financial education effective and how financial education impacts financial well-being.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\nFraud and scams present an ever-increasing problem for consumers, and no population is\nimmune. They affect young and older people, servicemembers, veterans, and their families.\nYoung adults are more frequent victims, while older adults are more heavily impacted financially\nwhen they are victims. Although the FTC4 and the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI)5\n4 See, e.g., Fed. Trade Comm’n., Consumer Advice, Top Scams of 2024, (Mar. 10, 2025),\nhttps://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2025/03/top-scams-2024.\n5 See, e.g., Fed. Bureau of Investigation, Internet Crime Report (2024),\nhttps://www.ic3.gov/AnnualReport/Reports/2024_IC3Report.pdf.\n11 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\nreceived fraud and scam reports totaling $12.5 to $16.6 billion in 2024, the true cost could be six\nor seven times higher, because most fraud and scams6 go unreported to federal agencies.\nConsumers are increasingly describing issues related to fraud and scams in their complaints to\nthe CFPB. For example, as peer-to-peer (P2P) payment platforms have flourished in recent years,\nso have fraud and scams. Between January 1, 2018, and December 31, 2023, the CFPB saw a\nsignificant increase in the number of complaints about P2P services; a machine learning model\nestimated that about 38% of the complaints were about fraud, amounting to millions of dollars\nstolen from consumers. Complaints include impersonation scams like “me to me” scams as well\nas unauthorized transfers that involve account takeover and identity theft. Consumers also\ncomplained about P2P companies’ responses to fraud and scams, including unresponsive\ncustomer service.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a decision evidence sufficiency brief for the fraud and scams evidence segment of the CFPB Financial Literacy Annual Report 2025. The purpose of this brief is to determine whether the segment's evidence is sufficient to support its claims about fraud and scam prevalence, P2P complaint trends, and population-specific impacts, and to identify any gaps or deficiencies that require remediation before the segment can be considered evidence-integrated.\n\nUse only the two supplied annual report sections and the sources they cite. Do not add external data, assumptions, or facts from outside the packet. Where a needed input is absent, preserve the missing status and label the result as Not Determinable.\n\nStructure the brief with the following level-2 headings in this order: 'Evidence Inventory', 'Evidence Sufficiency Assessment', 'Classification of Complaint Types', 'Flagged Gaps and Deficiencies', and 'Conclusion and Recommendation'.\n\nWrite the brief in a neutral, reviewer-facing tone suitable for an internal CFPB evidence-quality reviewer. Define abbreviations such as FTC, FBI, P2P, and CFPB on first use, and separate evidentiary findings from evaluative judgments.\n\nIn the 'Evidence Inventory' section, list each piece of evidence supplied in the two annual report sections, preserving exactly the source names, dates, URLs, dollar ranges, percentages, and report references as they appear. Include the FTC Protecting Older Consumers 2024-2025 citation dated Dec. 1, 2025, the FTC Consumer Sentinel Network Data Book dated March 2025, the FTC Top Scams of 2024 alert dated Mar. 10, 2025, the FBI Internet Crime Report 2024, the $12.5 to $16.6 billion range for fraud and scam reports in 2024, the six or seven times underreporting multiple, and the approximately 38% machine-learning estimate for P2P fraud complaints. Do not round or relabel any source value.\n\nIn the 'Evidence Sufficiency Assessment' section, distinguish between source-reported facts and inferences or estimates. Label each statement as Source Fact, Estimate, or Inference. For example, label the FTC and FBI fraud and scam report totals of $12.5 to $16.6 billion in 2024 as Source Fact, and label the machine learning model's estimate that about 38% of P2P complaints were about fraud as Estimate. Assess whether each claim in the segment is supported by a source-reported fact, an estimate, or an inference, and state whether the supporting evidence is sufficient.\n\nIn the 'Classification of Complaint Types' section, classify each fraud and scam complaint type mentioned in the segment—impersonation scams such as 'me to me' scams and unauthorized transfers involving account takeover and identity theft—into the categories of Confirmed, Unresolved, or Cannot Determine based on whether the supplied annual report sections provide sufficient supporting evidence for that complaint type. For each classification, cite the specific passage from the supplied text that supports it.\n\nIn the 'Flagged Gaps and Deficiencies' section, flag each evidence gap, unresolved inconsistency, or deficiency in the fraud and scams segment. Include the underreporting problem that most fraud and scams go unreported to federal agencies, and the consumer complaints about P2P companies' unresponsive customer service. Classify each flagged item as Confirmed, Unresolved, or Dependent on Missing Evidence, and explain the evidentiary basis for that classification.\n\nIn the 'Conclusion and Recommendation' section, verify the time window for the P2P complaint trend claim by confirming that the supplied period January 1, 2018 through December 31, 2023 is consistently applied to the significant increase in P2P complaints. State whether the segment's evidence falls within or outside that stated window, and quantify any lateness or mismatch.\n\nAlso in the 'Conclusion and Recommendation' section, compare the relative fraud and scam impact on young adults versus older adults using the supplied comparison that young adults are more frequent victims while older adults are more heavily impacted financially when they are victims. Rank the two populations by frequency of victimization and by financial impact, showing the basis for each ranking from the supplied text.\n\nFinally, state whether the fraud and scams evidence segment is sufficiently evidence-integrated to support its claims, or whether remediation is required. Base this conclusion only on the supplied materials and the assessments performed in the preceding sections. If the supplied materials do not support a definitive conclusion, state Not Determinable and identify the specific missing evidence that would be needed.",
