    "work_product": "decision evidence sufficiency brief for cfpb financial literacy annual report 2025 evidence segment ctx010 — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n15 See e.g., Fed. Trade Comm’n, Protecting Older Consumers 2024-2025, (Dec. 1, 2025).\nhttps://www.ftc.gov/system/files/ftc_gov/pdf/P144400-OlderAdultsReportDec2025.pdf.\n16 See e.g., Fed. Trade Comm’n, Consumer Sentinel Network Data Book, (March (2025).\nhttps://www.ftc.gov/system/files/ftc_gov/pdf/csn-annual-data-book-2024.pdf.\n14 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\nAs mentioned in previous sections, the CFPB also offered training and informational webinars\nrelated to fraud and scams to provide financial education to specific populations, including older\nadults and servicemembers.\n15 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\n3. Optimizing and measuring\nfinancial education and financial\nwell-being\nFinEd develops and maintains a broad array of digital financial education resources, from youth\nfinancial education materials to the popular set of questions and answers, Ask CFPB. FinEd also\nengages with the research community to measure the effectiveness of financial education\nprograms and methods.\nTo understand the effectiveness of its financial education efforts, FinEd uses two types of\nevaluation: process and impact. Process metrics gauge the format and delivery of financial\neducation content. Impact metrics examine changes in outcomes, such as increases in financial\nwell-being scores or decreases in debt. Through both types of metrics, the CFPB learns about\nwhat makes financial education effective and how financial education impacts financial well-being.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\nFraud and scams present an ever-increasing problem for consumers, and no population is\nimmune. They affect young and older people, servicemembers, veterans, and their families.\nYoung adults are more frequent victims, while older adults are more heavily impacted financially\nwhen they are victims. Although the FTC4 and the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI)5\n4 See, e.g., Fed. Trade Comm’n., Consumer Advice, Top Scams of 2024, (Mar. 10, 2025),\nhttps://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2025/03/top-scams-2024.\n5 See, e.g., Fed. Bureau of Investigation, Internet Crime Report (2024),\nhttps://www.ic3.gov/AnnualReport/Reports/2024_IC3Report.pdf.\n11 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\nreceived fraud and scam reports totaling $12.5 to $16.6 billion in 2024, the true cost could be six\nor seven times higher, because most fraud and scams6 go unreported to federal agencies.\nConsumers are increasingly describing issues related to fraud and scams in their complaints to\nthe CFPB. For example, as peer-to-peer (P2P) payment platforms have flourished in recent years,\nso have fraud and scams. Between January 1, 2018, and December 31, 2023, the CFPB saw a\nsignificant increase in the number of complaints about P2P services; a machine learning model\nestimated that about 38% of the complaints were about fraud, amounting to millions of dollars\nstolen from consumers. Complaints include impersonation scams like “me to me” scams as well\nas unauthorized transfers that involve account takeover and identity theft. Consumers also\ncomplained about P2P companies’ responses to fraud and scams, including unresponsive\ncustomer service.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a decision evidence sufficiency brief for the CFPB financial literacy annual report 2025 evidence segment that discusses fraud and scams. Use only the two supplied CFPB annual report sections; label any conclusion that cannot be established from those sections as Not Determinable.\n\nStructure the brief with level-2 headings in this order: Evidence Inventory, Timing Assessment, Classification of Fraud Issues, Source-Status Review, and Sufficiency Findings.\n\nWrite for an internal CFPB reviewer who must decide whether the evidence segment is sufficient, using neutral, audit-oriented language that separates evidence, procedure, result, and conclusion for each assessment.\n\nIn the Evidence Inventory section, assess the control and governance coverage of the evidence segment by reviewing the CFPB's fraud-and-scam training and informational webinars for older adults and servicemembers, and the process and impact evaluation approach FinEd uses to measure financial education effectiveness. State which control or governance elements are evidenced and which are not. For every evidence-sufficiency finding, map the supporting material and exact figure or statement, including the FTC Protecting Older Consumers 2024-2025 citation, the FTC Top Scams of 2024 citation, and the FBI Internet Crime Report 2024 citation, and keep those source labels beside each finding rather than only at the end.\n\nIn the Timing Assessment section, test the time-window sufficiency of the P2P complaint evidence by identifying the January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2023 period, confirming that the cited fraud-complaint increase falls within that window, and stating whether the segment's fraud-and-scam characterization is timely relative to that period.\n\nIn the Classification of Fraud Issues section, classify each fraud-and-scam issue described in the P2P complaints, including impersonation scams like \"me to me\" scams and unauthorized transfers involving account takeover and identity theft, into the categories of Evidenced, Partially Evidenced, or Not Determinable based on whether the supplied materials support the claim.\n\nIn the Source-Status Review section, identify the source status of the FTC Consumer Advice Top Scams of 2024 and FBI Internet Crime Report 2024 citations as external agency reports cited by the CFPB, and state that they are cited references rather than CFPB-generated findings when assessing whether the segment's fraud-and-scam claims are internally supported.\n\nIn the Sufficiency Findings section, flag any evidence gap or unresolved inconsistency in the segment, including the complaint that P2P companies' responses to fraud and scams involved unresponsive customer service, and state whether that gap is confirmed, unresolved, or dependent on missing evidence. Also compare the relative evidentiary support for the claim that young adults are more frequent fraud victims while older adults are more heavily impacted financially, using the supplied comparison key, and rank the two population-specific claims by the strength of evidence available in the materials.",
