    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nSBA Form 1919 (02/2025) 5 \nPurpose of this form: \nThe purpose of this form is to collect information about the Small Business Applicant (“Applicant”) and its owners, the loan request, existing indebtedness, \ninformation about current or previous government financing, and certain other topics. The information also facilitates background checks as authorized by \nsection 7(a)(1)(B) of the Small Business Act, 15 U.S.C. 636(a)(1)(B). Submission of the requested information is required for SBA or the Lender to \ndetermine eligibility for financial assistance. Failure to submit the information would affect that determination. \nInstructions for completing this form: \nA separate SBA Form 1919 for each co-Applicant(e.g., EPC and OC) must be completed and signed by the authorized representative of the Applicant and \nsubmitted to your SBA Participating Lender. The amounts of the “Purpose of the loan” sections across all SBA Forms 1919 should equal the total amount \nof the loan request. Submission of the requested information is required to make a determination regarding eligibility for financial assistance. \nFor purposes of reporting NAICS Code, Applicants must match the business activity code provided on their IRS income tax filings, if applicable. \nAll parties listed below are considered Owners of the Applicant: \n• For a sole proprietorship, the sole proprietor; \n• For a partnership, all general partners, and all limited partners owning 20% or more of the equity of the firm; \n• For a corporation, all owners of 20% or more of the corporation; \n• For limited liability companies, all members owning 20% or more of the company; and \n• Any Trustor (if the Applicant is owned by a trust).\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an eligibility decision rule matrix for the borrower information evidence segment of SBA Form 1919 (02/2025), designed for a lender eligibility reviewer. Begin with a one-sentence statement of the governing condition: the borrower information evidence segment is satisfied only when the required SBA Form 1919 information is submitted for each co-Applicant and the required Owners are listed as specified in the form instructions. Use only the supplied SBA Form 1919 borrower information segment; do not add external SBA policy, lender practice, or assumptions, and label any condition that cannot be established from the supplied text as Not Determinable. State that the matrix is derived from the SBA Form 1919 (02/2025) purpose statement and instructions, which are procedural form requirements, and do not present them as a final eligibility determination or as rule text beyond the form itself. Define any abbreviations on first use and keep the language neutral and procedural. Cover these topics in the matrix: (1) the purpose and scope of the borrower information collection, including the collection of information about the Small Business Applicant and its owners, the loan request, existing indebtedness, current or previous government financing, and other topics, and the facilitation of background checks under section 7(a)(1)(B) of the Small Business Act, 15 U.S.C. 636(a)(1)(B); (2) the submission requirement, including that submission of the requested information is required for SBA or the Lender to determine eligibility for financial assistance and that failure to submit the information would affect that determination; and (3) the owner-definition rules, including the separate SBA Form 1919 requirement for each co-Applicant such as an EPC and OC, the requirement that each form be completed and signed by the authorized representative of the Applicant and submitted to the SBA Participating Lender, and the listed owner categories for sole proprietorships, partnerships, corporations, limited liability companies, and trusts. For each co-Applicant scenario, include a conditional trigger: if a separate SBA Form 1919 is not completed and signed by the authorized representative and submitted to the SBA Participating Lender, mark the eligibility-decision rule as not satisfied and state the missing submission condition. For each owner-category rule, classify the listed party type using the classes Included, Not Included, or Cannot Determine, based only on the form's owner definitions. Close with a single next-action line tied to the most material unresolved evidence requirement identified in the matrix.",
