    "work_product": "decision evidence sufficiency brief for communications sec marketing risk alert 2024 evidence segment ctx014 — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: Prohibitions:11]\n\nInclusion or exclusion of performance results or time periods in manners that were not fair and balanced.22 The staff observed advertisements that included or excluded certain performance results or presented performance time periods in manners that were not fair and balanced. For example:\n\n• Advertisements that did not disclose the time period or did not disclose whether the returns were calculated for the same time period as additional performance information included in the same advertisement.\n\n• Advertisements that included or excluded certain performance results in manners that were not fair and balanced, such as advertisements that included the performance of only realized investment information in the total net return figure and excluded unrealized investments.\n\nAdvertisements that were otherwise materially misleading.23 The staff observed advertisements that appeared to otherwise be materially misleading, such as presenting disclosures in an unreadable font on websites or in videos.\n\nIV. Conclusion\n\nIn sharing these staff observations, the Division encourages advisers to reflect upon their own practices, policies, and procedures and to implement any appropriate modifications to their training, supervisory, oversight, and compliance programs.\n\n22 See Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-1(a)(6).\n\n23 See Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-1(a)(7).\n\nThis Risk Alert is intended to highlight for firms risks and issues that Division staff has identified.\n\n[Continues under: IV. Conclusion]\n\nIn addition, this Risk Alert describes risks that firms may consider to (1) assess their supervisory, compliance, and/or other risk management systems related to these risks, and (2) make any changes, as may be appropriate, to address or strengthen such systems. Other risks besides those described in this Risk Alert may be appropriate to consider, and some issues discussed in this Risk Alert may not be relevant to a particular firm’s business. The adequacy of supervisory, compliance and other risk management systems can be determined only with reference to the profile of each specific firm and other facts and circumstances.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: Prohibitions:11]\n\n17 See Marketing Rule Adopting Release, supra note 1 at 163 (The Commission stated, “an adviser’s advertisement would be misleading if it indicates that the adviser is rated highly without disclosing that the rating is based solely on a criterion, such as assets under management, that may not relate to the quality of the investment advice.”).\n\n• Performance advertisements contained information that was misleading, such as:\n\no Benchmark index comparisons that did not define the index or provide sufficient context to enable an understanding of the basis for such comparison or disclose that the benchmark performance did not include reinvestment of dividends.18 o Performance presentations that contained: (1) outdated market data information only (e.g., market data from more than five years prior); or (2) investment products that were no longer available to clients and included lower investment costs than were available.\n\no Statements or presentations regarding: (1) advisers’ performance track record with securities that were not purchased by the advisers in a similar manner in their clients’ accounts; (2) claims that the advisers achieved above average performance results without clarifying that the advisers did not yet have clients or performance track records; and (3) investment recommendations containing performance information that did not include disclosures to provide context to the presentations, such as advertising performance during time periods when most investors would have experienced the advertised performance returns because of general market performance.19 Fair and balanced treatment of material risks or limitations.20 The staff observed advertisements that included statements about the potential benefits connected with the advisers’ services or methods of operation that did not appear to provide fair and balanced treatment of any material risks or material limitations associated with the potential benefits. For example, the staff observed advertisements on social media that highlighted performance information without also disclosing the material risks and limitations associated with the potential benefits.\n\nQUERY:\nProduce a decision evidence sufficiency brief for the communications section's marketing risk alert 2024 evidence segment. The brief must assess whether the supplied evidence segment is sufficient for a decision on the communications section's marketing risk alert review.\n\nOpen the brief with a one-sentence statement of whether the supplied evidence segment is sufficient for the communications decision, and close with a `Next action:` line tied to the highest-priority unresolved deficiency.\n\nPresent the assessment in a structured evidence-sufficiency matrix with columns: `Observed Deficiency`, `Governing Standard`, `Evidence Status`, `Classification`, and `Remediation Need`. Include exactly three numbered findings, ordered from most to least decision-critical based on the deficiency categories present in the supplied text.\n\nUse only the supplied Risk Alert sections. Do not add external market data, customary practice, legal assumptions, or facts remembered from outside the packet. Any conclusion not supportable by the supplied materials must be marked `Not Determinable`.\n\nFor each observed deficiency from the performance-advertisement section, classify it as `Supported`, `Unsupported`, or `Not Determinable` based on whether the supplied text provides sufficient evidence for that deficiency category. Flag each observed deficiency as a confirmed gap, potential gap, or unresolved inconsistency based on whether the supplied text contains the specific deficiency description and the governing standard needed to establish it.\n\nFor every classification and remediation step, cite the exact staff-observed deficiency phrase and the governing standard or source-status language from the supplied Risk Alert sections, with source labels beside every entry in the matrix. End-only citations are insufficient.\n\nState explicitly that the Risk Alert is staff guidance identifying observed risks, not a rule or current obligation, and keep that non-binding status attached to every conclusion drawn from it. Identify which staff-observed advertising practices would violate fair-and-balanced or materially-misleading boundaries under the Marketing Rule references, without converting the Risk Alert's observations into a determination that any specific advertisement is non-compliant.\n\nCover the supervisory, training, oversight, and compliance program modifications the Risk Alert encourages advisers to consider, separated into control design versus operating-evidence considerations. For each classification gap identified, specify the next evidence-collection or follow-up action needed to close that gap.",
    "context_count": 2,
    "target_constraint_count": 12,
