    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nApril 17, 2024\n\nInitial Observations Regarding Advisers Act Marketing Rule Compliance*\n\nI. Introduction\n\nThe Division of Examinations is issuing this Risk Alert to provide investment advisers, investors, and other market participants with information regarding investment advisers’ compliance with amended Rule 206(4)-1 (the “Marketing Rule”) under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (“Advisers Act”).1 While the Division continues to focus on advisers’ compliance with the Marketing Rule,2 the staff is sharing these preliminary observations to encourage accurate completion of the Marketing Rule items contained in Form ADV and to promote compliance with Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-7 (the “Compliance Rule”), Advisers Act Rule 204-2 (the “Books and Records Rule”), and the Marketing Rule’s “General Prohibitions.”3\n\nII. Observations Regarding Compliance Rule, Books and Records Rule and Form ADV\n\nThe staff reviewed whether investment advisers had adopted and implemented written policies and procedures reasonably designed to prevent violations by the advisers and their supervised persons of the Advisers Act and the rules thereunder, including the Marketing Rule.4 * This Risk Alert represents the views of the staff of the Division of Examinations (the “Division”). This Risk Alert is not a rule, regulation, or statement of the Securities and Exchange Commission (the “SEC” or the “Commission”). The Commission has neither approved nor disapproved the content of this Risk Alert. This Risk Alert, like all staff statements, has no legal force or effect; it does not alter or amend applicable law, and it creates no new or additional obligations for any person.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an advertising review criterion matrix for investment adviser marketing communications based solely on the SEC Division of Examinations Risk Alert dated April 17, 2024, titled \"Initial Observations Regarding Advisers Act Marketing Rule Compliance.\"\n\nBegin the matrix with a one-sentence statement that this Risk Alert is staff guidance with no legal force or effect, and end with a one-sentence note that the matrix reflects only the preliminary observations contained in the Risk Alert and does not create new obligations.\n\nPresent the matrix as a table with the following columns: Advertising Review Criterion, Supporting Risk Alert Language, Applicable Rule Reference, Source Status, and Policy or Procedure Implication. Each row must represent one distinct criterion derived from the Risk Alert.\n\nCover the following named topics as distinct criterion areas in the matrix: compliance with amended Rule 206(4)-1 (the Marketing Rule) under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940, accurate completion of the Marketing Rule items contained in Form ADV, compliance with Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-7 (the Compliance Rule), compliance with Advisers Act Rule 204-2 (the Books and Records Rule), and the Marketing Rule's General Prohibitions.\n\nFor every criterion row, cite the specific language from the Risk Alert that supports the criterion, identifying the exact provision or observation and the corresponding rule reference. Preserve the exact rule references and named provisions from the Risk Alert—specifically amended Rule 206(4)-1 (the Marketing Rule), Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-7 (the Compliance Rule), Advisers Act Rule 204-2 (the Books and Records Rule), and the Marketing Rule's General Prohibitions—wherever they appear as governing standards in the matrix.\n\nInclude in the matrix a section covering the written policies and procedures observations from the Risk Alert, specifically the staff's review of whether investment advisers had adopted and implemented written policies and procedures reasonably designed to prevent violations by the advisers and their supervised persons of the Advisers Act and the rules thereunder, including the Marketing Rule. Map each advertising review criterion to the corresponding policy or procedure expectation where the Risk Alert supports such a mapping.\n\nState in the matrix that the Risk Alert is not a rule, regulation, or statement of the Securities and Exchange Commission, has no legal force or effect, does not alter or amend applicable law, and creates no new or additional obligations for any person. Ensure that no criterion is presented as a binding requirement.\n\nUse only the supplied Risk Alert text as the source for all criteria, rule references, and observations. Label any criterion area that cannot be established from the Risk Alert as Not Determinable rather than supplementing from outside knowledge.",
