    "work_product": "recommendation basis evidence matrix for suitability sec reg bi guide evidence segment ctx009 — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: What are the material facts relating to the scope and terms of the relationship with the retail customer that must be disclosed?]\n\nOther material facts relating to the type and scope of services provided to the retail customer, and that must be disclosed, include: whether or not you will monitor the retail customer’s account and the scope and frequency of any account monitoring services that you agree to provide; and whether you have any requirements for retail customers to open or maintain an account or establish a relationship, such as a minimum account size.\n\nwhether or not you will monitor the retail customer’s account and the scope and frequency of any account monitoring services that you agree to provide; and\n\nwhether you have any requirements for retail customers to open or maintain an account or establish a relationship, such as a minimum account size.\n\nOther material facts relating to the scope and terms of the relationship with the retail customer that must be disclosed include: general basis for your recommendations (i.e., what might commonly be described as your investment approach, philosophy, or strategy); and risks associated with your recommendations in standardized terms.\n\ngeneral basis for your recommendations (i.e., what might commonly be described as your investment approach, philosophy, or strategy); and\n\nrisks associated with your recommendations in standardized terms.\n\nAdditionally, you must consider, based on the facts and circumstances, whether there are other material facts relating to the scope and terms of the relationship with the retail customer that need to be disclosed.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: Special considerations for agreed-upon account monitoring]\n\nAgreed-upon monitoring: If you agree with a retail customer to perform account monitoring services, you are taking on an obligation to review and make recommendations with respect to that account (e.g., to buy, sell or hold) on the specified, periodic basis that you have agreed to with the retail customer. For example, if you agree to monitor your retail customer’s account on a quarterly basis, the quarterly review and each resulting recommendation to purchase, sell, or hold will be a recommendation subject to Regulation Best Interest.\n\nImplicit hold recommendations: If you have agreed to perform account monitoring services, then Regulation Best Interest applies even where you remain silent (i.e., an implicit hold recommendation).\n\nVoluntary account review: You may voluntarily, and without any agreement with your customer, review the holdings in your retail customer’s account for the purposes of determining whether to provide a recommendation to the customer. This voluntary review is not considered to be “account monitoring,” nor would it in itself create an implied agreement with the retail customer to monitor the customer’s account.\n\nAny explicit recommendation made to your retail customer as a result of any such voluntary review would be subject to Regulation Best Interest.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a recommendation basis evidence matrix for the scope and terms of the retail customer relationship under Regulation Best Interest using only the two supplied guidance sections. Begin with a one-sentence statement of which disclosure factors and monitoring classifications the supplied guidance supports.\n\nUse only the two supplied guidance sections; do not import external regulatory interpretations, industry practice, or remembered facts. Mark any profile factor or monitoring term that the supplied text does not establish as Not Determinable.\n\nCover the retail-customer relationship profile factors that the disclosure obligation makes relevant: whether account monitoring is agreed, the scope and frequency of any agreed monitoring, any minimum account size or account-maintenance requirement, the general basis for recommendations, and the risks associated with recommendations in standardized terms. Treat these as distinct topics in the matrix alongside the agreed-upon monitoring obligation and its periodic basis, implicit hold recommendations, voluntary account review, and the catch-all facts-and-circumstances disclosure requirement.\n\nFor each disclosure factor and each monitoring classification, map the supporting sentence from the two supplied sections, and keep that source mapping beside every classification, trigger result, and time-window output in the matrix; end-only citations are insufficient.\n\nClassify each retail-customer arrangement described in the matrix as Agreed-upon Monitoring, Voluntary Account Review, or Not Determinable using the distinctions in the account monitoring section, and separately classify each resulting recommendation as Subject to Regulation Best Interest or Not Determinable. Use only those labels in the arrangement classification field and only Subject to Regulation Best Interest and Not Determinable in the recommendation coverage field.\n\nIf the arrangement is classified as Agreed-upon Monitoring, then treat the specified periodic review and every resulting buy, sell, or hold recommendation, including silence as an implicit hold, as subject to Regulation Best Interest. If the arrangement is classified as Voluntary Account Review, then mark only explicit recommendations resulting from that review as subject to Regulation Best Interest.\n\nFor the quarterly monitoring example, test whether the agreed review period is quarterly by extracting the stated period from the account monitoring section, and show the source period, the applicable review basis, and whether the text establishes a quarterly cadence or leaves the actual agreed period Not Determinable.\n\nState that both supplied sections are SEC staff guidance and do not present them as rule text or as creating obligations beyond what the guidance itself describes; keep that status note attached to the matrix rather than converting the guidance into standalone requirements.\n\nClose with a Next action: line tied to the highest-priority factor or classification that remains Not Determinable under the supplied text.",
