    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: A. Compliance Rule]\n\n• Were updated to reflect the Marketing Rule but were not implemented. For example, the staff observed advisers’ policies that required net of fees performance to be included with any performance advertisement; however, the staff observed those same advisers including only gross performance in advertisements.\n\nB. Books and Records Rule\n\nThe staff observed that advisers typically had updated their policies and procedures to reflect Marketing Rule-related books and records maintenance and preservation requirements.\n\nNevertheless, the staff observed Marketing Rule-related books and records deficiencies, including:\n\n• Advisers completed questionnaires or surveys used in the preparation of a third-party rating but did not maintain a copy of such questionnaires.7\n\n• Advisers did not maintain copies of information posted to social media.8\n\n• Advisers did not maintain documentation to support performance claims included in advertisements.9\n\nC. Observations Related to Form ADV\n\nThe staff observed, at the time of their examinations, that many advisers had updated their Form ADVs, including: (1) Part 1A, Item 5.L related to advertising; and (2) Part 2A, Item 14 brochure disclosures related to advertising (e.g., client referrals and other compensation), when applicable.\n\nHowever, the staff also observed Marketing Rule-related deficiencies on Form ADV, such as advisers that inaccurately reported on Form ADV, Part 1A, that their advertisements did not include:\n\n• Third-party ratings, when their websites included third-party ratings or social media posts that touted the firms as being ranked in certain third-party ratings.\n\n• Performance results, when performance results were included in their marketing materials.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a marketing policy coverage brief for the communications section of the SEC Marketing Rule risk alert excerpt. The brief must report the alert's staff observations as findings, not as firm conclusions, and must avoid recommending corrective action beyond what the alert itself describes.\n\nPresent the brief as a table with these columns: Alert Section, Observed Deficiency or Gap, Affected Marketing Practice, and Boundary or Recordkeeping Implication.\n\nCover the alert's three substantive areas as separate topics: (1) Compliance Rule implementation gaps, (2) Books and Records Rule deficiencies, and (3) Form ADV reporting inaccuracies. Do not merge these areas into a single undifferentiated list.\n\nFor each row, map the observation to the specific alert section and practice it comes from, and keep that source location beside the deficiency, gap classification, and boundary implication rather than citing the alert only once at the end.\n\nFor each deficiency, classify it as a confirmed gap, unresolved inconsistency, or record-dependent gap. Use the alert's own distinction between policies that were updated but not implemented and policies that were never reflected in practice. Preserve the alert's exact wording for the net-of-fees versus gross performance discrepancy, the three named books and records deficiencies, and the two Form ADV misreporting categories; do not paraphrase them into broader compliance conclusions.\n\nIdentify which observed advertisement practices would fail the Marketing Rule's substantiation or fair presentation boundaries because the alert shows advisers circulating performance, third-party ratings, or social media content without the supporting records or disclosures their own policies required.\n\nList the specific records the alert identifies as missing or inadequately maintained, including third-party rating questionnaires, social media postings, and performance-claim documentation, and state the retention implication for each.\n\nFinally, for every deficiency, connect the alert's observed practice to the specific Marketing Rule-related requirement it implicates and then to the resulting boundary or recordkeeping action, so no observation appears as an isolated fact.",
