    "task": "Advertising review criterion mapping",
    "work_product": "advertising review criterion matrix for communications sec marketing risk alert 2024 evidence segment ctx011",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: Prohibitions:11]\n\n• Advertisements that contained untrue or misleading performance claims, including: (1) advertising cumulative profits that the advisers believed were not achievable or were impossible to achieve without unlimited money to invest, (2) presenting performance information that did not provide adequate disclosure regarding the share classes included in the performance returns, (3) using lower fees in calculations for net of fees performance returns than were offered to the intended audience, and (4) omitting material information regarding fees and expenses used in calculating returns.\n\n• Advertisements cited SEC registration beyond factual statements as to advisers’ registration status in a way to imply that SEC registration was representative of a particular level of skill or ability, or that the SEC had either approved or passed upon the advisers’ business practices. The staff also observed advisers including the SEC logo on their websites with the purpose of implying that the websites or the advisers had been approved or endorsed by the SEC.\n\n• Advertisements contained third-party ratings: (1) implying the advisers were the sole top recipients of certain awards when the awards went to multiple recipients or the advisers were not the top recipients; and (2) indicating that the advisers were highly rated by various organizations without disclosing that the methodologies for such ratings were based primarily or solely on factors that were not related to the quality of investment advice, such as assets under management, the number of clients, or that adviser personnel nominated fellow employees for such awards.17\n\n• Advertisements included testimonials that were misleading. For example, advisers included testimonials from clients of a third-party product on the advisers’ websites without any disclosures explaining the context of the testimonials, implying that the testimonials were about the advisers’ services rather than the third-party product.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an advertising review criterion matrix for the communications SEC marketing risk alert 2024 evidence segment. The supplied material is SEC staff guidance in the form of a risk alert and must be treated as non-binding staff observations rather than rule text or current legal requirements. Use only the supplied risk alert segment as the source of observed deficiencies and review criteria; do not introduce external advertising rules, regulatory requirements, or examples not present in the supplied text, and label any criterion that cannot be established from the supplied text as `Not Determinable`. Present the result as a matrix table with columns `Observed Deficiency`, `Mapped Review Criterion`, `Communication Boundary`, `Source Term Preserved`, and `Gap Status`. Write in a neutral, compliance-reviewer tone suitable for an internal advertising review function, defining each source term on first use and avoiding promotional or conclusory language. The matrix must cover, as distinct topics, each observed deficiency category in the supplied risk alert segment: untrue or misleading performance claims, SEC registration references, third-party ratings, and testimonials. For each mapped criterion, identify the communication-boundary restriction that prevents the observed deficiency from being circulated in an advertisement, such as not presenting staff observations as approved claims, not implying SEC endorsement, and not presenting unsupported ratings or testimonials as substantiated. Preserve the source's exact terms and phrases when mapping deficiencies to criteria, including 'untrue or misleading performance claims,' 'SEC registration,' 'third-party ratings,' 'testimonials,' 'share classes,' 'net of fees performance returns,' and 'assets under management.' For each mapped criterion, flag the gap status as `Confirmed Deficiency`, `Unresolved`, or `Dependent on Missing Evidence` based solely on whether the supplied risk alert segment explicitly identifies the deficiency, and identify any deficiency whose supporting disclosure or substantiation is absent from the supplied text as a gap.",
