    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nApril 17, 2024\n\nInitial Observations Regarding Advisers Act Marketing Rule Compliance*\n\nI. Introduction\n\nThe Division of Examinations is issuing this Risk Alert to provide investment advisers, investors, and other market participants with information regarding investment advisers’ compliance with amended Rule 206(4)-1 (the “Marketing Rule”) under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (“Advisers Act”).1 While the Division continues to focus on advisers’ compliance with the Marketing Rule,2 the staff is sharing these preliminary observations to encourage accurate completion of the Marketing Rule items contained in Form ADV and to promote compliance with Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-7 (the “Compliance Rule”), Advisers Act Rule 204-2 (the “Books and Records Rule”), and the Marketing Rule’s “General Prohibitions.”3\n\nII. Observations Regarding Compliance Rule, Books and Records Rule and Form ADV\n\nThe staff reviewed whether investment advisers had adopted and implemented written policies and procedures reasonably designed to prevent violations by the advisers and their supervised persons of the Advisers Act and the rules thereunder, including the Marketing Rule.4 * This Risk Alert represents the views of the staff of the Division of Examinations (the “Division”). This Risk Alert is not a rule, regulation, or statement of the Securities and Exchange Commission (the “SEC” or the “Commission”). The Commission has neither approved nor disapproved the content of this Risk Alert. This Risk Alert, like all staff statements, has no legal force or effect; it does not alter or amend applicable law, and it creates no new or additional obligations for any person.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a regulatory obligation register for the Marketing Rule evidence segment of the April 17, 2024 Division of Examinations Risk Alert titled \"Initial Observations Regarding Advisers Act Marketing Rule Compliance.\" Write the register for a compliance officer who needs to distinguish current obligations from non-binding staff observations; define each regulatory citation on first use and keep the register neutral and precise.\n\nUse only the supplied Risk Alert text. Do not add external legal requirements, customary practice, or facts remembered from outside the packet. Where a rule area or obligation cannot be established from the supplied text, enter `Not Determinable` rather than estimating it.\n\nPresent the register as a table with the following columns: `Rule Area`, `Obligation Extracted from Risk Alert`, `Source-Status Caveat`, and `Supporting Evidence Segment`. For every row, map the entry to the exact excerpt from the governing standard that introduces the corresponding rule area or obligation, and keep the source label beside each entry.\n\nCover the following named rule areas as introduced in the governing standard: amended Rule 206(4)-1 (the \"Marketing Rule\") under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (\"Advisers Act\"), Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-7 (the \"Compliance Rule\"), Advisers Act Rule 204-2 (the \"Books and Records Rule\"), and the Marketing Rule's \"General Prohibitions.\" Preserve the exact rule citations, rule names, and the April 17, 2024 date wherever they control an obligation entry.\n\nFor each entry, state in the `Source-Status Caveat` column that the Risk Alert is staff guidance that is not a rule, regulation, or statement of the SEC; that the Commission has neither approved nor disapproved its content; that it has no legal force or effect; that it does not alter or amend applicable law; and that it creates no new or additional obligations for any person. Keep this caveat distinct from the cited rules and regulations.\n\nAlso include as a governance coverage item the staff's review focus described in the Risk Alert: whether investment advisers had adopted and implemented written policies and procedures reasonably designed to prevent violations by the advisers and their supervised persons of the Advisers Act and the rules thereunder, including the Marketing Rule. Map this item to the supporting excerpt and apply the same source-status caveat.",
