    "item_id": "test300_supp_034",
    "workflow": "Risk and Compliance Review",
    "task": "Exception and threshold analysis and cross-provision requirements analysis",
    "work_product": "exception and threshold brief for risk disclosure sec plain english evidence segment ctx027 — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: The Elements of Style]\n\nWe provide information to you about our notes in three separate documents that progressively provide more detail: 1) the prospectus,\n\n2) the prospectus supplement, and 3) the pricing supplement. Since the terms of speciﬁc notes may differ from the general information we have provided, in all cases rely on information in the pricing supplement over different information in the prospectus and the prospectus supplement; and rely on this prospectus supplement over different information in the prospectus.\n\n28 a plain english handbook or We provide information to you about our notes in three separate documents that progressively provide more detail: 1 The Prospectus General information that may or may not apply to each note.\n\n2 The Prospectus Supplement\n\nMore speciﬁc than the prospectus, and to the extent information differs from the prospectus, rely on the different information in this document.\n\n3 The Pricing Supplement\n\nProvides ﬁnal details about a speciﬁc note including its price. To the extent information differs from the prospectus or the prospectus supplement, rely on the different information in this document.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: 3 The Pricing Supplement]\n\nInformation-packed sentences leave most investors scratching their heads. So many of these sentences have become “boilerplate” that writers cut and paste them into new documents without thinking about how they can be improved. Since these sentences can be a little intimi­ dating, we thought we’d tackle another one: before The Drake Capital Corporation (the “Company”) may offer from time to time its Global Medium-Term Notes, Series A, Due from 9 months to 60 Years From Date of Issue, which are issuable in one or more series (the “Notes”), in the United States in an aggre­ gate principal amount of up to U.S. $6,428,598,500, or the equiva­ lent thereof in other currencies, including composite currencies such as the European Currency Unit (the “ECU”) (provided that, with respect to Original Issue Discount Notes (as defined under “Description of Notes—Original Issue Discount Notes”), the initial offering price of such Notes shall be used in calculating the aggre­ gate principal amount of Notes offered hereunder).\n\nThe Drake Capital Corporation may offer at various times up to U.S. $6,428,598,500 worth of Global Medium-term notes. These notes will mature from 9 months to 60 years af ter the date they are purchased. We will offer these notes in series, starting with Series A, and in U.S., foreign, and composite currencies, like the European Currency Unit. If we offer original issue discount notes, we will use their initial offering prices to calculate when we reach $6,428,598,500.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an exception and threshold brief for the risk disclosure SEC plain English evidence segment. Use the two supplied passages only: the three-document hierarchy passage and the Drake Capital Corporation offering passage.\n\nStructure the brief with level-2 headings `Precedence Rule`, `Threshold Test`, `Maturity Window`, `Exception Classification`, and `Open Items` in that order.\n\nUnder `Precedence Rule`, state the controlling hierarchy among the prospectus, the prospectus supplement, and the pricing supplement. Preserve the document names exactly as they appear in the source.\n\nUnder `Threshold Test`, identify the aggregate principal amount cap stated in the Drake Capital Corporation passage. Preserve the exact figure U.S. $6,428,598,500, including commas and no decimal places. Compare any stated offering amount against that cap and state whether the cap is reached, exceeded, or not reached. For original issue discount notes, state the stated basis for calculating the aggregate principal amount.\n\nUnder `Maturity Window`, state the maturity range from the passage. Test whether each stated maturity falls within the 9-month to 60-year window from the date of purchase. Classify each maturity as `Within Window`, `Outside Window`, or `Cannot Determine`.\n\nUnder `Exception Classification`, classify each identified disclosure term as `Consistent`, `Overridden`, or `Cannot Determine` under the three-document precedence hierarchy. If a term in the prospectus or prospectus supplement differs from the pricing supplement, mark it `Overridden` and identify the pricing supplement term that controls. If a term in the prospectus differs from the prospectus supplement, mark it `Overridden` and identify the prospectus supplement term that controls.\n\nMap every classification and threshold result to the exact document and passage from which it comes. Cite the specific passage beside each result, not only at the end.\n\nUnder `Open Items`, list any term, amount, or maturity for which the supplied passages do not permit a determination. State which additional document or passage would be needed to resolve each open item.\n\nKeep the full brief between 220 and 320 whitespace-separated words.",
