    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: 2. What recommendations are covered?]\n\nWe interpret whether a recommendation has been made to a retail customer that triggers the best interest obligation consistent with precedent under the anti-fraud provisions of the federal securities laws as applied to broker-dealers and with how the term has been applied under the rules of self-regulatory organizations (such as FINRA).\n\nRegulation Best Interest does not apply to investment advice provided to a retail customer by a dual-registrant when acting in the capacity of an investment adviser, even if the retail customer has a brokerage relationship with the dual-registrant or the dual-registrant executes the transaction in a brokerage capacity. [2]\n\nAccount recommendations include recommendations of securities account types generally (e.g., to open an IRA or other brokerage account), as well as recommendations to roll over or transfer assets from one type of account to another (e.g., a workplace retirement plan account to an IRA). As discussed more below, special considerations exist where the financial professional making the recommendation is dually-registered.\n\nAny securities transaction or investment strategy involving securities includes : explicit hold recommendations; and implicit hold recommendations that are the result of agreed-upon account monitoring between the broker-dealer and retail customer. Special considerations for providing agreed-upon account monitoring are discussed more below.\n\nexplicit hold recommendations; and\n\nimplicit hold recommendations that are the result of agreed-upon account monitoring between the broker-dealer and retail customer. Special considerations for providing agreed-upon account monitoring are discussed more below.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: 1. Introduction]\n\nCompliance Obligation : establish, maintain, and enforce written policies and procedures reasonably designed to achieve compliance with Regulation Best Interest.\n\nRecord-making and Recordke e ping : You must also comply with new record-making and recordkeeping requirements.\n\n2. What recommendations are covered?\n\nRegulation Best Interest applies to recommendations of any securities transaction or investment strategy involving securities (including account recommendations).\n\nWhat is a recommendation? The determination of whether a broker-dealer has made a recommendation that triggers application of Regulation Best Interest turns on the facts and circumstances of a particular situation, and therefore, whether a recommendation has been made is not susceptible to a bright line definition. Factors considered in determining whether a recommendation has taken place include whether the communication “reasonably could be viewed as a ‘call to action’” and “reasonably would influence an investor to trade a particular security or group of securities.” The more individually tailored the communication to a specific customer or targeted group of customers about a security or group of securities, the greater the likelihood that the communication may be viewed as a “recommendation.” We interpret whether a recommendation has been made to a retail customer that triggers the best interest obligation consistent with precedent under the anti-fraud provisions of the federal securities laws as applied to broker-dealers and with how the term has been applied under the rules of self-regulatory organizations (such as FINRA). Regulation Best Interest does not apply to investment advice provided to a retail customer by a dual-registrant when acting in the capacity of an investment adviser, even if the retail customer has a brokerage relationship with the dual-registrant or the dual-registrant executes the transaction in a brokerage capacity. [2]\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a disclosure control assessment for the Regulation Best Interest recommendation-coverage segment using only the supplied guide sections. Structure the assessment with the following sections in this order: Scope and Source Materials, Recommendation Coverage Analysis, Control Design Assessment, Classification of Coverage Elements, Evidence-to-Rule Chain, and Closing Readiness Gate. Write for a compliance officer reviewing disclosure controls: define each Regulation Best Interest term on first use and pair every control conclusion with the governing guide provision that supports it.\n\nIn the Recommendation Coverage Analysis, cover these named topics: securities transaction or investment strategy involving securities; account recommendations; rollover or transfer recommendations; explicit hold recommendations; implicit hold recommendations resulting from agreed-upon account monitoring; dual-registrant capacity limits; and the facts-and-circumstances recommendation standard. Preserve the exact source language for the call to action test, the individually tailored communication factor, the dual-registrant capacity limitation, the account recommendation examples, and the compliance obligation wording without paraphrasing or altering their regulatory scope.\n\nIn the Control Design Assessment, assess the compliance obligation to establish, maintain, and enforce written policies and procedures reasonably designed to achieve compliance with Regulation Best Interest, separating policy design from evidence that such policies and procedures operated during the relevant period. Also address the record-making and recordkeeping requirements as a control design element.\n\nIn the Classification of Coverage Elements, classify each covered communication or account recommendation as Triggering, Non-Triggering, or Cannot Determine based on the supplied guide language concerning account types, rollovers, dual-registrant capacity, and hold recommendations. Apply this conditional trigger: if a communication reasonably could be viewed as a call to action and reasonably would influence an investor to trade a particular security or group of securities, classify the communication as triggering the Regulation Best Interest best interest obligation; otherwise retain a non-triggering classification and state the missing condition. For any element that cannot be established from the supplied guide sections, use Not Determinable rather than importing external Regulation Best Interest interpretations or customary broker-dealer practice.\n\nMap each control conclusion and classification to the exact guide provision that supplies it, placing the source label beside every classification, every trigger test, and every control design finding rather than using end-only citations. In the Evidence-to-Rule Chain section, for each classification and control finding, show the active guide evidence, the governing compliance obligation or recordkeeping requirement it supports, and the resulting disclosure control action in one connected chain rather than as separate facts, rules, and conclusions.\n\nIdentify the supplied materials as staff guidance rather than rule text, and do not present the compliance obligation, recordkeeping duty, or recommendation coverage statements as current enforceable requirements beyond their guidance status. Close with a one-sentence readiness gate stating which disclosure control element still lacks sufficient evidence before the compliance obligation can be confirmed as reasonably designed and enforced.",
