    "workflow": "Risk and Compliance Review",
    "task": "Escalation trigger extraction and cross-section evidence analysis",
    "work_product": "escalation trigger register for communications sec marketing risk alert 2024 evidence segment ctx005 — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: A. Compliance Rule]\n\n• Were updated to reflect the Marketing Rule but were not implemented. For example, the staff observed advisers’ policies that required net of fees performance to be included with any performance advertisement; however, the staff observed those same advisers including only gross performance in advertisements.\n\nB. Books and Records Rule\n\nThe staff observed that advisers typically had updated their policies and procedures to reflect Marketing Rule-related books and records maintenance and preservation requirements.\n\nNevertheless, the staff observed Marketing Rule-related books and records deficiencies, including:\n\n• Advisers completed questionnaires or surveys used in the preparation of a third-party rating but did not maintain a copy of such questionnaires.7\n\n• Advisers did not maintain copies of information posted to social media.8\n\n• Advisers did not maintain documentation to support performance claims included in advertisements.9\n\nC. Observations Related to Form ADV\n\nThe staff observed, at the time of their examinations, that many advisers had updated their Form ADVs, including: (1) Part 1A, Item 5.L related to advertising; and (2) Part 2A, Item 14 brochure disclosures related to advertising (e.g., client referrals and other compensation), when applicable.\n\nHowever, the staff also observed Marketing Rule-related deficiencies on Form ADV, such as advisers that inaccurately reported on Form ADV, Part 1A, that their advertisements did not include:\n\n• Third-party ratings, when their websites included third-party ratings or social media posts that touted the firms as being ranked in certain third-party ratings.\n\n• Performance results, when performance results were included in their marketing materials.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: II. Observations Regarding Compliance Rule, Books and Records Rule and Form ADV]\n\nThis Risk Alert does not address all observed deficiencies related to the Marketing Rule. In addition, deficiencies described in this Risk Alert may also be deficiencies under other parts of the Marketing Rule or under the Advisers Act.\n\n4 See Marketing Rule Adopting Release, su pra note 1 at footnote 112 (The Commission stated that “Advisers should address their marketing practices in their policies and procedures under the compliance rule.”) See also id. a 239 (The Commission\n\nA. Compliance Rule\n\nThe staff observed that advisers’ compliance policies and procedures typically included processes to comply with the Marketing Rule. The staff also observed that advisers typically provided training for relevant staff on the Marketing Rule requirements and the advisers’ corresponding marketing policies and procedures. In general, advisers that updated their written marketing-related policies and procedures typically established a process for reviewing advertisements. Many of these advisers also required preapproval of advertisements before dissemination.5 The staff, however, observed instances where advisers’ policies and procedures were not reasonably designed or implemented to address compliance with the Marketing Rule, which resulted in gaps for preventing violations of the Marketing Rule, Books and Records Rule, or both. For example, the staff observed policies and procedures that:\n\n• Consisted only of general descriptions and expectations related to the Marketing Rule.6\n\n• Did not address applicable marketing channels utilized by the advisers, such as websites and social media.\n\n• Were informal rather than in writing.\n\n• Were incomplete, not updated, or partially updated for certain applicable marketing topics.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an escalation trigger register for the 2024 SEC Risk Alert evidence segment covering Marketing Rule compliance deficiencies. The register should open with a short 'Scope and Source Status' paragraph stating that this Risk Alert is staff guidance, does not address all observed deficiencies related to the Marketing Rule, and that deficiencies described may also be deficiencies under other parts of the Marketing Rule or under the Advisers Act. Then present the register as a table with the following columns: Trigger Category, Observed Deficiency, Governing Basis, Required Record, Control or Governance Gap, Deficiency Classification, Marketing Communication Boundary, and Escalation Condition. For each trigger row, use the exact source terms for the deficiency categories, including 'net of fees performance', 'gross performance', 'third-party ratings', 'questionnaires or surveys', 'social media', and 'Form ADV, Part 1A'. For each row, identify the required record or documentation that would evidence compliance, such as copies of third-party rating questionnaires, copies of social media posts, or documentation supporting performance claims. For each row, classify the observed deficiency as a confirmed gap, a potential gap requiring additional evidence, or an unresolved inconsistency between policy and practice. For each row, connect the cited observed deficiency to the governing Marketing Rule or Books and Records Rule requirement and then to the escalation condition that would follow from that deficiency. For each row, flag whether the underlying deficiency involves a marketing communication practice that would require substantiation or fair presentation before circulation, such as gross-only performance where the policy requires net-of-fees performance. For each row, identify the control or governance gap associated with the trigger, such as policies not implemented, policies not addressing marketing channels, or missing preapproval processes. Mark any trigger category where the Risk Alert indicates it does not address all observed deficiencies or where the cited evidence is incomplete as 'Unresolved' rather than confirmed or complete. After the table, include a 'Cross-Section Evidence Gaps' section that identifies any trigger category where the two supplied Risk Alert sections provide incomplete or non-overlapping evidence and states what additional evidence would be needed to close the gap. Write the register for a compliance officer preparing an internal risk alert response, using precise Marketing Rule terminology and distinguishing observed deficiencies from governing standards.",
