    "item_id": "test300_supp_044",
    "workflow": "Risk and Compliance Review",
    "task": "Compliance testing evidence specification and cross-section evidence analysis",
    "work_product": "compliance testing evidence plan for communications sec marketing risk alert 2024 evidence segment ctx007 — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: III. Observations Regarding Compliance with the Marketing Rule’s General Prohibitions]\n\n• Referencing specific investment advice provided by the adviser in a manner that is not fair and balanced.\n\n• Including or excluding performance results, or presenting performance time periods, in a manner that is not fair and balanced.\n\n• Providing information that is otherwise materially misleading.\n\nA. Observations Related to the Marketing Rule’s General Prohibitions The staff observed the following deficiencies related to the Marketing Rule’s General\n\nProhibitions:11\n\nUntrue statements of material fact12 and unsubstantiated statements of material fact.13 The staff observed advertisements that included statements of material fact that appeared to be untrue. In 10 See Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-1(a).\n\n11 See Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-1(a). While the staff references the General Prohibitions, these findings may be violations of multiple prohibitions in the Marketing Rule and other provisions of the Advisers Act.\n\n12 See Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-1(a)(1).\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: C. Observations Related to Form ADV]\n\n• Hypothetical performance, when hypothetical performance was included in advertisements.\n\nThe staff also observed advisers using outdated language in their Form ADVs referencing provisions of the prior Cash Solicitation Rule (Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-3), inaccurately 7 See Advisers Act Rule 204-2(a)(11)(ii).\n\n8 See Advisers Act Rule 204-2(a)(11)(i).\n\n9 See Advisers Act Rule 204-2(a)(16).\n\nindicating that no referral arrangements existed, and omitting material terms and compensation of referral arrangements on Form ADV, Part 2A, Item 14.\n\nIII. Observations Regarding Compliance with the Marketing Rule’s General Prohibitions\n\nThe staff’s review for Marketing Rule compliance assessed whether advisers that advertise violated any of the following General Prohibitions:10\n\n• Including an untrue statement of a material fact or omitting a material fact necessary to make the statement made, in light of the circumstances under which it was made, not misleading.\n\n• Including a material statement of fact that the adviser does not have a reasonable basis for believing it will be able to substantiate upon demand by the Commission.\n\n• Including information that would reasonably be likely to cause an untrue or misleading implication or inference to be drawn concerning a material fact relating to the adviser.\n\n• Discussing any potential benefits to clients or investors connected with or resulting from the adviser’s services or methods of operation without providing fair and balanced treatment of any associated material risks or limitations.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a compliance testing evidence plan for the communications segment of the SEC Marketing Risk Alert 2024 evidence review. The plan must cover only the two supplied SEC staff guidance sections: the observations regarding compliance with the Marketing Rule's General Prohibitions and the observations related to Form ADV. Open the plan with a one-sentence scope statement identifying the Marketing Rule General Prohibitions and Form ADV observations as the testing focus, then use level-2 headings in this order: Scope and Source Status, Deficiency-to-Rule Mapping, Evidence Requests by Topic, Control Design, and Readiness Gate.\n\nUnder Scope and Source Status, label the SEC staff observations as non-binding staff guidance, not as rule text or current enforcement findings, and state that the staff observations may indicate violations of multiple Marketing Rule prohibitions or other Advisers Act provisions without asserting a definitive violation. Restrict all evidence requests and testing conclusions to the two supplied SEC staff guidance sections; mark any deficiency category or control expectation not supported by those materials as Not Determinable rather than importing external regulatory practice.\n\nUnder Deficiency-to-Rule Mapping, build a table that connects each staff-observed deficiency from the supplied materials to the applicable General Prohibition text from Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-1(a) and to the specific evidence request or testing action that would verify whether the deficiency exists in the adviser's communications. For each row, cite the exact governing standard from Advisers Act Rule 206(4)-1(a) and the corresponding staff-observed deficiency, keeping the rule citation beside the evidence request rather than in an end-only footnote. Present each row as one linked chain of staff-observed deficiency, applicable rule text, and evidence request, not as separate lists.\n\nUnder Evidence Requests by Topic, require distinct coverage of four topics, each with its own evidence request: fair and balanced treatment of specific investment advice, performance presentation fairness, untrue or unsubstantiated statements of material fact, and Form ADV accuracy regarding referral arrangements and hypothetical performance. For each evidence request, classify it as Received, Missing, or Unresolved Match against the corresponding staff-observed deficiency, and classify each deficiency category as Covered by Rule Text, Staff Observation Only, or Not Determinable based on whether the supplied materials contain the governing standard. Identify which observed deficiency categories, such as untrue statements of material fact or unsubstantiated statements of material fact, would render an advertisement non-compliant for marketing circulation, and specify that the plan must not treat hypothetical performance, outdated Form ADV language, or omitted referral arrangement terms as acceptable for external distribution.\n\nUnder Control Design, separate preventive controls, such as pre-publication review of advertisements against the General Prohibitions, from detective controls, such as periodic review of Form ADV language and referral arrangement disclosures, and name the control owner responsible for each evidence request. Close the plan with a one-sentence evidence-readiness gate listing which evidence categories remain unverified before testing can begin.",
