    "work_product": "incentive conflict mitigation compliance requirement mapping brief — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nWhat are some potential mitigation measures?\n\nThe following non-exhaustive list of practices could be used as potential mitigation methods for broker-dealers to comply with the mitigation requirement:\n\navoiding compensation thresholds that disproportionately increase compensation through incremental increases in sales;\n\nminimizing compensation incentives for employees to favor one type of account over another; or to favor one type of product over another, proprietary or preferred provider products, or comparable products sold on a principal basis, for example, by establishing differential compensation based on neutral factors;\n\neliminating compensation incentives within comparable product lines by, for example, capping the credit that an associated person may receive across mutual funds or other comparable products across providers;\n\nimplementing supervisory procedures to monitor recommendations that are: near compensation thresholds; near thresholds for broker-dealer recognition; involve higher compensating products, proprietary products or transactions in a principal capacity; or, involve the roll over or transfer of assets from one type of account to another (such as recommendations to roll over or transfer assets in an ERISA account to an IRA) or from one product class to another;\n\nnear thresholds for broker-dealer recognition;\n\ninvolve higher compensating products, proprietary products or transactions in a principal capacity; or,\n\ninvolve the roll over or transfer of assets from one type of account to another (such as recommendations to roll over or transfer assets in an ERISA account to an IRA) or from one product class to another;\n\nadjusting compensation for associated persons who fail to adequately manage conflicts of interest; and\n\nlimiting the types of retail customer to whom a product, transaction or strategy may be recommended.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: 7. What does the Compliance Obligation require?]\n\nIn addition to the required policies and procedures, depending on your size and complexity, a reasonably designed compliance program generally would also include:\n\nremediation of non-compliance;\n\n8. Record-making and Recordkeeping\n\nYou must meet new record-making and recordkeeping requirements with respect to certain information collected from or provided to retail customers in connection with Regulation Best Interest. This builds upon existing record-making and recordkeeping obligations.\n\nFor each retail customer to whom a recommendation of any securities transaction or investment strategy involving securities is or will be provided, you must keep a record of all information collected from and provided to the retail customer pursuant to Regulation Best Interest, as well as the identity of each natural person who is an associated person, if any, responsible for the account.\n\nYou must retain all records of the information collected from or provided to each retail customer for at least six years after the earlier of the date the account was closed or the date on which the information was replaced or updated.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an incentive conflict mitigation compliance requirement mapping brief as an evidence-integrated revision for a compliance officer or compliance committee audience. Define Regulation Best Interest as Reg BI on first use. Use only the two supplied materials: the non-exhaustive list of potential mitigation measures for broker-dealers and the continuing section on the Compliance Obligation and record-making and recordkeeping under Regulation Best Interest. Label any required conclusion, applicability determination, or retention end date that cannot be established from the supplied materials as 'Not Determinable' rather than supplementing with external regulatory knowledge or assumed compliance program facts.\n\nPresent the mapped requirements in exactly two sections, first the mitigation practices and then the recordkeeping obligations, with each section ordered by the source order of the supplied materials and every retention period stated in the source time unit.\n\nIn the first section, cover the named topics of compensation-threshold avoidance, account-type and product-type incentive minimization, supervisory monitoring of near-threshold and rollover recommendations, and remediation of non-compliance. For each mitigation practice, use the exact label 'Mitigation Requirement' and map the practice back to its exact source sentence, keeping the source label beside each mapped requirement. Preserve the source terms and qualifying language exactly, including the phrase 'non-exhaustive list', the named compensation-threshold and supervisory-procedure practices, and the distinction between avoiding compensation thresholds that disproportionately increase compensation through incremental sales and minimizing compensation incentives for employees to favor one type of account or product over another. Flag each mitigation practice as a compliance gap only when the supplied text shows the practice is absent from the compliance program, and otherwise classify it as 'Listed Practice' without treating the non-exhaustive list as a mandatory checklist.\n\nIn the second section, cover the record-making and recordkeeping obligations for information collected from or provided to retail customers. For each record obligation, use the exact label 'Recordkeeping Requirement' and map the obligation back to its exact source sentence, keeping the source label beside each mapped requirement. Cover the supervisory procedures needed to monitor recommendations near compensation thresholds, near broker-dealer recognition thresholds, involving higher-compensating or proprietary products, or involving rollovers or transfers, and connect those supervisory procedures to the recordkeeping requirement to capture the identity of each associated person responsible for the account. List the records that must be kept for each retail customer, including all information collected from and provided to the retail customer and the identity of each natural person who is an associated person responsible for the account, without inventing any record type not stated in the supplied materials.\n\nFor the six-year retention obligation, perform a time-window analysis by applying the 'earlier of the date the account was closed or the date on which the information was replaced or updated' trigger to each record category, and state the resulting retention period without assuming any account close or update date not supplied in the materials. Preserve the source terms exactly, including the six-year retention period and the 'earlier of' retention trigger. Where the supplied materials do not establish whether a particular mitigation practice or recordkeeping obligation is present in a specific compliance program, state 'Not Determinable' rather than drawing an unsupported conclusion.",
