    "work_product": "inaccurate claims and omitted material facts compliance review evidence specification brief",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: Prohibitions:11]\n\n• Advertisements describing material facts about advisory services or products offered that were inaccurate, including: (1) referencing certain investment mandates of the advisers in advertisements when there were no such mandates used by the firms (e.g., ESG mandates);14 (2) claiming that investment processes were validated by professional institutions when they were not; (3) stating that the adviser considered certain risk tolerances when recommending investment strategies when all clients were placed into the same strategy without consideration of risk tolerances; (4) referencing a list of approved securities that did not exist; (5) referencing formalized securities screening processes that did not exist; and (6) misrepresenting the advisers’ client base, such as describing the adviser as a “private fund adviser” when the firm did not advise any private funds.\n\n• Advertisements publicizing the receipt of certain awards or accolades that were not received.\n\nOmission of material facts or misleading inference. The staff observed advertisements that appeared to omit material facts necessary to make the statements made, in light of the circumstances under which they were made, not misleading.15 The staff also observed advertisements that included information that could have reasonably caused untrue or misleading implications or inferences to be drawn concerning material facts relating to the advisers. 16 For example: this requirement is met. However, if an adviser is unable to substantiate the material claims of fact made in an advertisement when the Commission demands it, we will presume that the adviser did not have a reasonable basis for its belief.”).\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a compliance review evidence specification brief for the marketing communications described in the supplied SEC staff risk alert. Use only the supplied risk alert text as your source; do not add external facts, assumptions, or examples, and label any result that cannot be established from the text as Not Determinable.\n\nFirst, state clearly that the source is staff-observed guidance from a risk alert, not a binding rule or an adjudicated violation. The alert describes observed deficiencies and a presumptive substantiation standard, and this non-binding status must remain visible wherever the brief relies on the source.\n\nFrom the risk alert, extract each enumerated example of an inaccurate statement about material facts and each category of omitted material fact or misleading inference. Preserve the alert's exact enumerated examples, including the reference to investment mandates such as ESG mandates when no such mandates were used, without paraphrasing them into different claims or adding examples not present in the text.\n\nFor each extracted claim or omission, map it to the exact governing standard language in the risk alert that makes the claim or omission a compliance concern, and keep that source mapping beside each finding rather than citing the alert only once at the end.\n\nClassify each extracted item as Confirmed Deficiency, Unresolved Inconsistency, Potential Gap with Missing Evidence, or Not Determinable, using only those labels. For each item, determine whether the claim is a material claim of fact that would trigger the substantiation expectation described in the alert, and record the resulting circulation restriction in a column labeled Circulation Restriction exactly once. Any claim that cannot be substantiated from the supplied text must be flagged as restricted from circulation rather than presented as current or guaranteed performance.\n\nPresent the findings in a table with columns for each advertisement claim, the supporting evidence, the observed inaccurate or omitted fact, the substantiation status, and the circulation restriction, so that each claim is traceable to its evidence and disposition.",
