    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: Entire Prospectus—Design]\n\n(d) ( ) To enhance the readability of the prospectus, you must use plain English principles in the organization, language, and design of the front and back cover pages, the summary, and the risk factors section.\n\n(2) You must draft the language in these sections so that at a mini­ mum it substantially complies with each of the following plain English writing principles:\n\n(ii) Definite, concrete, everyday words;\n\n(iii) Active voice;\n\n(iv) Tabular presentation or bullet lists for complex material,\n\n(v) No legal jargon or highly technical business terms; and\n\n(vi) No multiple negatives.\n\n(3) In designing these sections or other sections of the prospectus, you may include pictures, logos, charts, graphs, or other design elements so long as the design is not misleading and the required information is clear . You are encouraged to use tables, schedules, charts and graphic illustrations of the results of operations, balance sheet, or other financial data that present the data in an understandable manner. Any presentation must be consistent with the financial statements and non-financial information in the prospectus. You must draw the graphs and charts to scale. Any information you provide must not be misleading.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\nKaren Elizabeth Gordon, The T ransitive Vampire: A Handbook\n\nof Grammar for the Innocent, the Eager, and the Doomed (Times Books, 1984).\n\n_______, The N ew Well-Tempered Sentence, A Punctuation Handbook\n\nfor the Innocent, the Eager, and the Doomed (Ticknor & Fields, 1993).\n\nWilliam Lutz, The New Doublespeak: Why No One Knows\n\nWhat Anyone’s Saying Anymore\n\nDavid Mellinkoff, Legal W riting: Sense & Nonsense\n\n(West Publishing Company, 1982).\n\nWilliam Strunk, Jr., and E.B. White, Elements of Style\n\nacmillan, 3rd rev. ed., 1981).\n\nEdward R. Tufte, The V isual Display of Quantitative Information\n\nRobin Williams, The M ac Is Not a Typewriter\n\nRichard C. Wydick, Plain English for Lawyers\n\narolina Academic Press, 2nd ed., 1985).\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a prospectus plain English presentation compliance review evidence specification brief, written as an evidence-integrated revision for a compliance reviewer who will use it to check a draft prospectus against the governing plain English rule. The brief must be written in neutral, audit-ready language that distinguishes mandatory requirements from non-binding guidance and never addresses the issuer as a customer.\n\nBase the brief only on the two supplied materials. The first material contains the governing standard: it requires plain English principles in the organization, language, and design of the front and back cover pages, the summary, and the risk factors section, and it requires the language in those sections to substantially comply with the following writing principles: definite, concrete, everyday words; active voice; tabular presentation or bullet lists for complex material; no legal jargon or highly technical business terms; and no multiple negatives. The same material also permits pictures, logos, charts, graphs, or other design elements only if the design is not misleading and the required information is clear, and it requires that any presentation be consistent with the financial statements and non-financial information in the prospectus and that graphs and charts be drawn to scale. The second material lists grammar and style handbooks, including Karen Elizabeth Gordon, The Transitive Vampire: A Handbook of Grammar for the Innocent, the Eager, and the Doomed (Times Books, 1984); Karen Elizabeth Gordon, The New Well-Tempered Sentence, A Punctuation Handbook for the Innocent, the Eager, and the Doomed (Ticknor & Fields, 1993); William Lutz, The New Doublespeak: Why No One Knows What Anyone's Saying Anymore; David Mellinkoff, Legal Writing: Sense & Nonsense (West Publishing Company, 1982); William Strunk, Jr., and E.B. White, Elements of Style (Macmillan, 3rd rev. ed., 1981); Edward R. Tufte, The Visual Display of Quantitative Information; Robin Williams, The Mac Is Not a Typewriter; and Richard C. Wydick, Plain English for Lawyers (Carolina Academic Press, 2nd ed., 1985).\n\nStructure the brief so that it separates design requirements from operating evidence. For each of the three prospectus sections named in the rule, identify the drafting principles that apply to that section and state what evidence would show that each principle was applied during the relevant drafting period. For each drafting principle, identify who must review that section for compliance. Map each drafting principle and each design-element restriction to the exact rule provision that imposes it, and map each cited handbook to the specific drafting principle it supports, keeping the rule and guidance sources visibly separate so that the reviewer can trace every requirement to its source.\n\nPreserve the exact rule terms, section names, and drafting principles from the governing standard, and reproduce the handbook titles, authors, publishers, and publication years from the cited style references without altering or normalizing them. State explicitly that the governing standard is mandatory rule text while the cited grammar and style handbooks are non-binding reference guidance, and do not present the handbooks as imposing independent compliance obligations.\n\nFor each drafting principle and each design-element restriction, classify the item using only the labels Mandatory, Guidance, or Unresolved. If any of the five drafting principles lacks supporting evidence that it was applied to the front and back cover pages, the summary, and the risk factors section, label that principle Unresolved and state what evidence is needed to close the gap. Apply the design-element communication restriction so that any picture, logo, chart, graph, or other design element included in the prospectus is flagged as non-compliant if it is misleading or obscures required information, and state the circulation boundary for each flagged element. Do not use the words approved, compliant, or cleared for any item whose supporting evidence is missing, and label any drafting principle or design-element restriction that cannot be established from the supplied materials as Not Determinable rather than assuming customary practice.",
