    "work_product": "recommendation scope compliance review evidence specification brief — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: 2. What recommendations are covered?]\n\nWe interpret whether a recommendation has been made to a retail customer that triggers the best interest obligation consistent with precedent under the anti-fraud provisions of the federal securities laws as applied to broker-dealers and with how the term has been applied under the rules of self-regulatory organizations (such as FINRA).\n\nRegulation Best Interest does not apply to investment advice provided to a retail customer by a dual-registrant when acting in the capacity of an investment adviser, even if the retail customer has a brokerage relationship with the dual-registrant or the dual-registrant executes the transaction in a brokerage capacity. [2]\n\nAccount recommendations include recommendations of securities account types generally (e.g., to open an IRA or other brokerage account), as well as recommendations to roll over or transfer assets from one type of account to another (e.g., a workplace retirement plan account to an IRA). As discussed more below, special considerations exist where the financial professional making the recommendation is dually-registered.\n\nAny securities transaction or investment strategy involving securities includes : explicit hold recommendations; and implicit hold recommendations that are the result of agreed-upon account monitoring between the broker-dealer and retail customer. Special considerations for providing agreed-upon account monitoring are discussed more below.\n\nexplicit hold recommendations; and\n\nimplicit hold recommendations that are the result of agreed-upon account monitoring between the broker-dealer and retail customer. Special considerations for providing agreed-upon account monitoring are discussed more below.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\nWhen making an account type recommendation, does Regulation Best Interest or the Advisers Act apply?\n\nDually registered financial professionals: If you are a financial professional who is dually registered (i.e., an associated person of a broker-dealer and a supervised person of an investment adviser (regardless of whether you work for a dual-registrant, affiliated firm, or unaffiliated firm)) making an account recommendation to a retail customer, whether Regulation Best Interest or the Advisers Act applies will depend on the capacity in which you are acting when making the recommendation. If you are acting as a broker-dealer or associated person thereof, you must comply with Regulation Best Interest and will need to take into consideration all types of accounts that you offer (i.e., both brokerage and advisory accounts) when making the recommendation of an account that is in the retail customer’s best interest.\n\nIndividuals registered only as broker-dealers or associated persons : If you are only registered as an associated person of a broker-dealer (regardless of whether that broker-dealer entity is a dual-registrant or affiliated with an investment adviser), Regulation Best Interest will apply to that account recommendation, but you need to take into consideration only the brokerage accounts available. You can only recommend a brokerage account that the broker-dealer offers if you have a reasonable basis to believe that the recommended brokerage account is in the best interest of the retail customer, and the broker-dealer otherwise complies with Regulation Best Interest.\n\nQUERY:\nProduce a Recommendation Scope Compliance Review Evidence Specification Brief — Evidence-Integrated Revision. Use only the two supplied SEC staff guidance sections as the evidentiary basis; where the materials do not establish the registration status or capacity for a given recommendation scenario, mark the applicable standard as `Not Determinable` rather than inferring or supplying external facts.\n\nStructure the brief with level-2 headings in this order: `Purpose and Scope`, `Source Materials and Status`, `Coverage and Exclusion Rules`, `Capacity-Based Applicability Test`, `Evidence-to-Determination Mapping`, and `Open Items`.\n\nWrite in a neutral, reviewer-facing tone for a compliance or supervisory reviewer. Define `dual-registrant`, `associated person`, `supervised person`, and `account type recommendation` on first use, and avoid advocacy language about which standard should apply.\n\nIn `Source Materials and Status`, state explicitly that both supplied sections are SEC staff guidance interpreting Regulation Best Interest and are not rule text, and that the capacity-based applicability analysis is an interpretive framework rather than a current case-specific obligation for any named firm or individual.\n\nIn `Coverage and Exclusion Rules`, map each coverage and exclusion rule to the specific guidance section and provision that supports it. Use the section on what recommendations are covered for the definition of account recommendations, the dual-registrant exclusion, and hold recommendations, including the distinction between explicit hold recommendations and implicit hold recommendations resulting from agreed-upon account monitoring. Use the section on account type recommendations and the Advisers Act for the capacity-based test, the all-accounts consideration requirement for broker-dealer capacity, and the brokerage-only limitation for individuals registered only as associated persons of a broker-dealer.\n\nPreserve the exact terms, categories, and rule references from both guidance sections wherever they control the analysis, including `retail customer`, `dual-registrant`, `associated person of a broker-dealer`, `supervised person of an investment adviser`, `brokerage account`, `advisory account`, `account type recommendation`, `Regulation Best Interest`, and `Advisers Act`, without paraphrasing or substituting equivalent labels.\n\nIn `Capacity-Based Applicability Test`, require a determination, for each account recommendation scenario constructed from the supplied guidance, of whether Regulation Best Interest or the Advisers Act applies based on the financial professional's registration status and capacity when making the recommendation. Limit the permitted outcomes to `Regulation Best Interest applies`, `Advisers Act applies`, or `Not Determinable`.\n\nApply the following conditional rule: if the recommendation is made by a dually registered financial professional acting in the capacity of an investment adviser, identify the applicable standard as the Advisers Act and mark Regulation Best Interest as not applicable, even when the retail customer has a brokerage relationship or the transaction is executed in a brokerage capacity.\n\nIn `Evidence-to-Determination Mapping`, present each applicability determination as a connected chain showing the active evidence from the relevant guidance section, the governing capacity or registration rule extracted from that section, and the resulting applicable standard. Do not separate the capacity facts, the interpretive rule, and the determination into disconnected fragments.\n\nIn `Open Items`, list any scenario for which the supplied guidance does not establish the registration status or capacity, and state that the applicable standard remains `Not Determinable` for that scenario.",
