    "work_product": "conflict disclosure materiality compliance review evidence specification brief — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nWhat are the material facts relating to conflicts of interest associated with a recommendation that must be disclosed?\n\nFor purposes of Regulation Best Interest a “ conflict of interest ” is defined to mean “an interest that might incline a broker, dealer, or a natural person who is an associated person of a broker or dealer – consciously or unconsciously – to make a recommendation that is not disinterested.”\n\nSuch conflicts include, for example: conflicts associated with proprietary products, payments from third parties, and compensation arrangements.\n\nBroker-dealers must disclose all material facts relating to conflicts of interest associated with the recommendation.\n\nFor purposes of Regulation Best Interest, “material facts” is interpreted consistent with the standard articulated in Basic v. Levinson . [4] Accordingly, information is material if there is a “substantial likelihood that a reasonable shareholder would consider it important.” In the context of Regulation Best Interest, the standard is the retail customer, as defined in the rule.\n\nWhat is “full and fair” disclosure?\n\nThe Release contains guidance on what constitutes “ full and fair ” disclosure under Regulation Best Interest. Your obligation to provide full and fair disclosure should give sufficient information to enable a retail investor to make an informed decision with regard to the recommendation.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: Special considerations for agreed-upon account monitoring]\n\nAgreed-upon monitoring: If you agree with a retail customer to perform account monitoring services, you are taking on an obligation to review and make recommendations with respect to that account (e.g., to buy, sell or hold) on the specified, periodic basis that you have agreed to with the retail customer. For example, if you agree to monitor your retail customer’s account on a quarterly basis, the quarterly review and each resulting recommendation to purchase, sell, or hold will be a recommendation subject to Regulation Best Interest.\n\nImplicit hold recommendations: If you have agreed to perform account monitoring services, then Regulation Best Interest applies even where you remain silent (i.e., an implicit hold recommendation).\n\nVoluntary account review: You may voluntarily, and without any agreement with your customer, review the holdings in your retail customer’s account for the purposes of determining whether to provide a recommendation to the customer. This voluntary review is not considered to be “account monitoring,” nor would it in itself create an implied agreement with the retail customer to monitor the customer’s account.\n\nAny explicit recommendation made to your retail customer as a result of any such voluntary review would be subject to Regulation Best Interest.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a conflict disclosure materiality compliance review evidence specification brief using only the two supplied guidance sections. Begin with a one-sentence materiality posture for the conflict disclosure review. Then present the disclosure evidence specification as a table with the following columns: `Conflict Category`, `Governing Standard`, `Specific Evidence Required`, `Source Status`, and `Classification`. The brief must cover these named topics as distinct sections: conflict-of-interest definition, materiality standard, full-and-fair disclosure obligation, agreed-upon account monitoring, implicit hold recommendations, and voluntary account review. For every required disclosure element, map it back to the exact governing standard from the conflict-disclosure section and the account-monitoring section, and cite the specific figure, rule, or example used. Preserve the exact defined terms and legal references from the two sections, including the Basic v. Levinson materiality standard, the Regulation Best Interest conflict-of-interest definition, and the agreed-upon monitoring language, without paraphrasing or relabeling them. State that the conflict-disclosure and full-and-fair guidance is Release guidance rather than rule text, and that the account-monitoring section is guidance about how Regulation Best Interest applies, so the brief must not present either as a current rule obligation. Verify the quarterly monitoring cadence by stating the agreed periodic basis, the applicable review window, and the date-calculation rule for determining whether each review and resulting recommendation falls within the agreed quarterly period. Classify each conflict-disclosure evidence element as `Disclosure Required`, `Disclosure Not Required`, or `Cannot Determine` using only the supplied materiality standard and the account-monitoring trigger. For each classification, connect the active evidence from the account-monitoring facts, the governing disclosure standard from the conflict-disclosure section, and the resulting classification or evidence request into one chain. Use only the two supplied guidance sections and label any element that cannot be established from them as `Not Determinable` rather than importing outside facts or assumptions. Close with a one-sentence evidence-readiness gate stating which evidence is still required before the disclosure can be treated as complete.",
