    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: Are there specific factors a broker-dealer should consider when making account type recommendations, or recommendations to open an IRA, or to roll over assets into an IRA?]\n\nWhen making recommendations to open an IRA, or to roll over assets into an IRA , you should consider a variety of factors including, but not limited to: fees and expenses; level of services available; available investment options; ability to take penalty-free withdrawals; application of required minimum distributions; protections from creditors and legal judgments; holdings of employer stock; and any special features of the existing account.\n\nability to take penalty-free withdrawals;\n\napplication of required minimum distributions;\n\nprotections from creditors and legal judgments;\n\nholdings of employer stock; and\n\nany special features of the existing account.\n\nAre there special considerations when broker-dealers recommend securities or investment strategies that are complex or risky?\n\nWhen recommending securities or investment strategies that are complex, such as inverse or leveraged exchange-traded products, you should take particular care to make sure you understand the terms, features, and risks – as with the potential risks, rewards, and costs of any security or investment strategy – in order to establish a reasonable basis to recommend the product to retail customers. Further, you must weigh the potential risks, rewards, and costs of the particular product or investment strategy, in light of the particular retail customer’s investment profile.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: 5. What does the Care Obligation require?]\n\nhave a reasonable basis to believe that a series of recommended transactions, even if in the retail customer’s best interest when viewed in isolation, is not excessive and is in the retail customer’s best interest when taken together in light of the retail customer’s investment profile.\n\nWhether you have complied with the Care Obligation will be evaluated as of the time of the recommendation (and not in hindsight).\n\nWhat does the first component of the Care Obligation require?\n\nYou must exercise reasonable diligence, care, and skill to understand the potential risks, rewards, and costs associated with the recommendation.\n\nWhat would constitute reasonable diligence, care, and skill will vary depending on, among other things, the complexity of and risks associated with the recommended security or investment strategy and the broker-dealer’s familiarity with the recommended security or investment strategy.\n\nWhile every inquiry will be specific to the particular broker-dealer and the recommended security or investment strategy, you generally should consider important factors such as:\n\nthe security’s or investment strategy’s: investment objectives; characteristics (including any special or unusual features); liquidity; volatility; and likely performance in a variety of market and economic conditions;\n\ncharacteristics (including any special or unusual features);\n\nlikely performance in a variety of market and economic conditions;\n\nthe expected return of the security or investment strategy; and\n\nany financial incentives to recommend the security or investment strategy.\n\nTogether, this inquiry should allow you to develop a sufficient understanding of the security or investment strategy and to be able to reasonably believe that it could be in the best interest of at least some retail customers.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an evidence-integrated compliance review evidence specification brief for the IRA rollover and complex product review. Open with a one-sentence overall evidence-readiness status for this review, and close with a one-sentence `Next action:` line identifying the highest-priority evidence gap or unresolved review condition.\n\nOrganize the brief into the following required review topics, each mapped to the governing guidance factor: (1) IRA and rollover account-type factors; (2) complex or risky product diligence; (3) Care Obligation diligence factors; and (4) the customer investment profile linkage. For each topic, identify the specific risk or impact the guidance requires the reviewer to weigh, including the potential risks, rewards, and costs of the particular product or investment strategy in light of the particular retail customer's investment profile, and separately distinguish what the guidance directs the reviewer to consider from any limitation in the supplied guidance itself.\n\nSpecify the customer profile evidence the reviewer must obtain and document, including the particular retail customer's investment profile referenced in the IRA rollover and complex product guidance, and mark any profile fields that the supplied guidance does not define as needing a separate source.\n\nFor each review topic, apply a conditional evidence-status label: if the required account-type factor, complex-product diligence item, or customer profile input is supported by the supplied guidance, label it `Evidence specified`; otherwise label it `Evidence required`. Do not label a topic `Evidence specified` while any required input remains unsupported, and use `Not determinable` where the supplied guidance does not permit a complete readiness determination.\n\nFor the IRA and rollover account-type review, compare the supplied account-type factors against the existing-account special features and the complex-product considerations, stating for each supported factor its benefit, limitation, and implication for the reviewer's evidence request.\n\nWrite the brief for a compliance reviewer or audit reviewer: define each guidance term on first use, distinguish governing guidance from implementation steps, and pair every required review topic with the specific guidance factor that controls it.\n\nFor every required review topic, present the evidence-rule-action chain in sequence: the governing guidance factor from the supplied sections, the evidence the reviewer must collect for that factor, and the resulting readiness or follow-up action. Do not separate the factor, evidence, and action into disconnected lists.\n\nDo not state that a review topic is complete or that compliance with the Care Obligation is established while the as-of-recommendation evaluation rule requires evidence that the supplied guidance does not itself provide. Where the necessary case facts are absent, permit only a conditional readiness statement or `Not determinable`.\n\nPreserve the exact factor names, terms, and evaluation timing from the supplied guidance wherever they control the evidence specification, including the IRA and rollover factor list, the complex-product terms, and the as-of-recommendation evaluation rule.\n\nUse only the labels `Evidence specified`, `Evidence required`, and `Not determinable` for the readiness status of each review topic, and use the exact phrase `Next action:` exactly once in the closing line.\n\nIdentify both supplied materials as SEC staff guidance rather than rule text, state that they do not by themselves establish a broker-dealer's compliance in any particular matter, and do not present the guidance factors as a current obligation to produce a specific case outcome.",
