    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: § 240.17a-4 Records to be preserved by certain exchange members, brokers and dealers.]\n\n(a) Every member, broker or dealer subject to § 240.17a-3 must preserve for a period of not less than 6 years, the first two years in an easily accessible place, all records required to be made pursuant to § 240.17a-3(a)(1) through (3), (5), and (21) and (22), and analogous records created pursuant to § 240.17a-3(e).\n\n(b) Every member, broker or dealer subject to § 240.17a-3 must preserve for a period of not less than three years, the first two years in an easily accessible place:\n\n(1) All records required to be made pursuant to § 240.17a-3(a)(4), (6) through (11), (16), (18) through (20), and (25) through (31), and analogous records created pursuant to § 240.17a-3(e).\n\n(2) All check books, bank statements, cancelled checks and cash reconciliations.\n\n(3) All bills receivable or payable (or copies thereof), paid or unpaid, relating to the member, broker or dealer's business as such.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: § 240.17a-3 Records to be made by certain exchange members, brokers and dealers.]\n\n(a) Every member of a national securities exchange who transacts a business in securities directly with others than members of a national securities exchange, every broker or dealer who transacts a business in securities through the medium of any such member, and every broker or dealer registered pursuant to section 15 of the Act (15 U.S.C. 78 o ) must make and keep current the following books and records relating to its business:\n\n(1) Blotters (or other records of original entry) containing an itemized daily record of all purchases and sales of securities (including security-based swaps), all receipts and deliveries of securities (including certificate numbers), all receipts and disbursements of cash and all other debits and credits. Such records must show the account for which each such purchase or sale was effected, the name and amount of securities, the unit and aggregate purchase or sale price, if any (including the financial terms for security-based swaps), the trade date, and the name or other designation of the person from whom such securities were purchased or received or to whom sold or delivered. For security-based swaps, such records must also show, for each transaction, the type of security-based swap, the reference security, index, or obligor, the date and time of execution, the effective date, the scheduled termination date, the notional amount(s) and the currenc(ies) in which the notional amount(s) is expressed, the unique transaction identifier, and the counterparty's unique identification code.\n\n(2) Ledgers (or other records) reflecting all assets and liabilities, income and expense and capital accounts.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare an operational sign-off checklist for the records covered by § 240.17a-4, using only the supplied text of § 240.17a-3 and § 240.17a-4. Begin with a one-sentence readiness statement naming the highest-risk record category or preservation obligation requiring attention before sign-off. Then present exactly six checklist rows in a Markdown table with columns for Record Category, Making Requirement, Preservation Period, Accessibility Condition, Evidence, and Sign-off Status. Order the rows from the six-year preservation category in § 240.17a-4(a) to the three-year categories in § 240.17a-4(b)(1) through (3). For each row, identify the record category, the record-making duty under § 240.17a-3(a), the applicable preservation period from § 240.17a-4, and the first-two-years easily-accessible condition. In the Evidence column, cite the specific § 240.17a-3 paragraph and the specific § 240.17a-4 paragraph supporting that checklist line, keeping the source labels beside every record category, retention period, accessibility condition, and sign-off result. Preserve the exact source terms for the six-year period, the three-year period, the first two years in an easily accessible place, and every record category cross-reference from § 240.17a-3(a) and § 240.17a-4(b) without rounding, relabeling, or substituting analogous wording. For each row, apply the following conditional sign-off rule: mark the row as Cleared only if the record category is both made and kept current under § 240.17a-3 and preserved for the applicable period with the first two years in an easily accessible place under § 240.17a-4; otherwise mark it as Hold and identify the missing condition. For each row, also state whether the category falls under the six-year window in § 240.17a-4(a) or the three-year window in § 240.17a-4(b), including the first-two-years easily-accessible sub-period for every category. Distinguish the record-making and current-maintenance control in § 240.17a-3 from the preservation and accessibility control in § 240.17a-4, and name the sign-off owner for each gap identified in either control. Label any record category or sign-off condition that cannot be established from the supplied materials as Not Determinable rather than supplying an external assumption. End with a Next action line tied to the most significant unresolved sign-off condition.",
