    "work_product": "transitional confirmation provisions and exemptions trade confirmation requirements mapping brief — evidence-integrated revision",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: § 240.10b-10 Confirmation of transactions.]\n\n(2) Transitional provision. (i) Broker-dealers are not required to comply with paragraph (e)(1)(iii) of this section until June 1, 2003, Provided that, if, not withstanding the absence of the disclosure required in that paragraph, the broker-dealer receives a written request from a customer for the information described in paragraph (e)(1)(iii) of this section, the broker-dealer must make the information available to the customer; and\n\n(ii) Broker-dealers are not required to comply with paragraph (e)(1)(iv) of this section until June 1, 2003.\n\n(f) The Commission may exempt any broker or dealer from the requirements of paragraphs (a) and (b) of this section with regard to specific transactions of specific classes of transactions for which the broker or dealer will provide alternative procedures to effect the purposes of this section; any such exemption may be granted subject to compliance with such alternative procedures and upon such other stated terms and conditions as the Commission may impose.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: § 240.10b-10 Confirmation of transactions.]\n\n(C) To advise each customer in the group if a payment is not received from the designated person on behalf of the group within 10 days of a date certain specified in the arrangement for delivery of that payment by the designated person and thereafter to send to each such customer the written notification described in paragraph (a) of this section for the next three succeeding payments.\n\n(7) NMS stock shall have the meaning provided in § 242.600 of this chapter.\n\n(8) Payment for order flow shall mean any monetary payment, service, property, or other benefit that results in remuneration, compensation, or consideration to a broker or dealer from any broker or dealer, national securities exchange, registered securities association, or exchange member in return for the routing of customer orders by such broker or dealer to any broker or dealer, national securities exchange, registered securities association, or exchange member for execution, including but not limited to: research, clearance, custody, products or services; reciprocal agreements for the provision of order flow; adjustment of a broker or dealer's unfavorable trading errors; offers to participate as underwriter in public offerings; stock loans or shared interest accrued thereon; discounts, rebates, or any other reductions of or credits against any fee to, or expense or other financial obligation of, the broker or dealer routing a customer order that exceeds that fee, expense or financial obligation.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a compliance implementation memo that maps the transitional confirmation provisions and the exemption authority in the supplied excerpts from § 240.10b-10 Confirmation of transactions, using only the two supplied rule-text excerpts and no external regulatory interpretation, industry practice, or remembered requirements. Use the exact label 'Not Determinable' for any requirement whose applicability cannot be established from the supplied text, and use the exact phrase 'Transitional Provision' only when referring to paragraph (2) of § 240.10b-10.\n\nOrganize the memo into exactly four numbered sections in this order, and within each section present the governing text, the applicable condition or deadline, and the resulting obligation or exemption in that order:\n\n1. Transitional Provision for paragraph (e)(1)(iii): Quote the controlling phrase from the supplied text and cite the specific subsection letter or paragraph number beside the mapped requirement. Identify the source date June 1, 2003 and state whether the obligation is currently deferred or triggered. If a customer's written request for the information described in paragraph (e)(1)(iii) is present, state that the broker-dealer must make the information available despite the absence of the disclosure required in that paragraph; if no such written request is present, state that the transitional non-compliance period continues until June 1, 2003.\n\n2. Transitional Provision for paragraph (e)(1)(iv): Quote the controlling phrase and cite the specific subsection letter or paragraph number beside the mapped requirement. Identify the source date June 1, 2003 and state that broker-dealers are not required to comply with paragraph (e)(1)(iv) until that date.\n\n3. Commission Exemption Authority under paragraph (f): Quote the controlling phrase and cite the specific subsection letter or paragraph number beside the mapped requirement. State that any exemption under paragraph (f) requires Commission action and is subject to compliance with alternative procedures and stated terms and conditions; do not describe a broker-dealer as exempt unless the Commission has granted such an exemption.\n\n4. Payment for Order Flow Definition and 10-Day Customer-Advice Obligation: Quote the controlling phrase and cite the specific subsection letter or paragraph number beside the mapped requirement. Classify each payment-for-order-flow example in the definition as either an enumerated example expressly listed in the definition or a general category captured by the opening clause, using only the supplied text. For the 10-day customer-advice obligation, identify the date certain and the 10-day window without performing any external date calculation.\n\nIdentify both supplied excerpts as current rule text from § 240.10b-10 and do not describe the transitional provisions as expired or the Commission's exemption authority as self-executing. Preserve the exact dates, subsection references, and defined terms as they appear in the supplied text, including 'June 1, 2003', 'paragraph (e)(1)(iii)', 'paragraph (e)(1)(iv)', and '10 days of a date certain'. Define 'payment for order flow' and 'NMS stock' on first use, and keep each section actionable for internal procedures.",
