    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\nFraud Risks in Federal Programs.\nFraud Risk Governance, (2) Fraud Risk Management, (3) Fraud Control Activities, (4) Fraud\nInvestigation and Corrective Action, and (5) Fraud Monitoring Activities to determine\nenhancements to the CFPB’s fraud risk management activities. The evaluation highlighted areas\nof focus for the CFPB as it continues its efforts to develop a framework of anti-fraud practices.\nLimitations of the financial statements\nThe financial statements contained in this report have been prepared to present the financial\nposition and results of operations of the CFPB pursuant to the requirements of the Dodd-Frank\nAct Section 1017(a)(4)(B). While the statements have been prepared from the books and records\nof the CFPB, in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles for the Federal\nGovernment, and follow the general presentation guidance provided by OMB, the statements\nare prepared using the same books and records. The financial and performance data in this\nreport are reliable and complete. The statements should be read with the understanding that\nthey are for a component of the U.S. Government, a sovereign entity.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\nSystem (CSRS), or CSRS Offset); however, a few transferees from OTS are in a non-Title 5 plan\n(i.e., Pentegra Defined Benefit Plan). Transferees from the Federal Reserve were allowed to\nremain in the Federal Reserve System retirement program or to affirmatively elect into the\nappropriate Title 5 retirement plan during that same timeframe. For those employees who\nelected to enroll in an alternative retirement plan, the enrollment became effective in January\n2013.\nThe CFPB does not report on its financial statements’ information pertaining to the retirement\nplans covering its employees. Reporting amounts such as plan assets, accumulated plan\nbenefits, and related unfunded liabilities, if any, is the responsibility of the Federal Reserve\nSystem, OCC, or OPM as the administrator of their respective plans. In all cases, the CFPB pays\nany employer contributions required by the plans. Refer to the chart below for information on\nwhich agency administers each of the retirement plans for the CFPB employees.\nOCC, OTS, and FDIC also offered other agency-only savings plans to employees. Any transferees\nwho participated in such plans are allowed to continue their participation as long as they remain\nenrolled in their current retirement plans. In such cases, the CFPB pays any employer\ncontributions. Employees who elect to enroll in the Federal Reserve System retirement plan will\nnot be allowed to continue their participation in either the Title 5 Thrift Savings Plan or the\nOCC, OTS, and FDIC agency savings plans.\nThe CFPB has also reimbursed the transferring agencies for administrative costs pursuant to\nMOU with the transferring agencies. These costs are reflected as expenses in the CFPB’s\nfinancial statements.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a financial statement scope and limitations reporting scope and controls analysis brief for the CFPB's financial statements. The brief must explain which retirement-plan information is excluded from the CFPB's financial statements and which conditions change that scope, using the two supplied source sections as the controlling evidence.\n\nStart by applying the January 2013 enrollment effective date from the retirement-plan election language to determine which alternative retirement plan elections fall within the reporting scope of the CFPB financial statements and which fall outside because they became effective before the CFPB prepared its statements from its own books and records.\n\nThroughout the brief, preserve the exact source terms, plan names, agency names, and governing references wherever they control the scope conclusion, including the Dodd-Frank Act Section 1017(a)(4)(B) basis, the Pentegra Defined Benefit Plan label, the Title 5 Thrift Savings Plan and OCC, OTS, and FDIC agency savings plans labels, and the Federal Reserve System retirement program election language.\n\nUse the exact label \"Reporting scope status:\" once for the overall scope conclusion, and use only \"In scope\", \"Out of scope\", and \"Conditional\" as the permitted status values for each retirement-plan category.\n\nCover the fraud risk governance and control activities from the CFPB's fraud risk management evaluation together with the retirement-plan administrator responsibilities assigned to the Federal Reserve System, OCC, or OPM, and separate the CFPB's employer-contribution payment obligation from the administrators' reporting obligation for plan assets, accumulated plan benefits, and unfunded liabilities.\n\nState that the financial statements are prepared from the CFPB's books and records under the Dodd-Frank Act Section 1017(a)(4)(B) requirement and that the statements are for a component of the U.S. Government, a sovereign entity, and distinguish this governing basis from the separate statement that reporting amounts for retirement plans is the responsibility of the Federal Reserve System, OCC, or OPM as administrator.\n\nApply the conditional trigger that an employee who elects to enroll in the Federal Reserve System retirement plan will not be allowed to continue participation in the Title 5 Thrift Savings Plan or the OCC, OTS, and FDIC agency savings plans, and require the brief to classify each affected plan category as \"Conditional\" when that election condition is present.\n\nCover the following named topics for the retirement-plan reporting scope: the non-Title 5 Pentegra Defined Benefit Plan for OTS transferees, the Federal Reserve System retirement program election option, the Title 5 retirement plan election option, and the restriction on continuing the Thrift Savings Plan or agency savings plans after electing the Federal Reserve System retirement program.\n\nPresent the retirement-plan scope analysis as a table with columns \"Plan category\", \"Administrator\", \"CFPB reporting obligation\", \"Election condition\", and \"Reporting scope status:\".\n\nIdentify the Federal Reserve System, OCC, or OPM as the administrator responsible for reporting plan assets, accumulated plan benefits, and related unfunded liabilities, and require the brief to state that the CFPB's employer-contribution payment obligation does not transfer that reporting responsibility to the CFPB.\n\nReturn exactly four plan categories in the scope table, ordered from most clearly out of scope to most clearly in scope, and keep the narrative portion of the brief to no more than 180 words.\n\nCompare the two retirement-plan alternatives available to Federal Reserve transferees: remaining in the Federal Reserve System retirement program or affirmatively electing into the appropriate Title 5 retirement plan, and state the reporting consequence of each alternative for the CFPB's financial statements.\n\nFor every plan category in the scope table, cite the exact source language that establishes the administrator and the election condition, including the Pentegra Defined Benefit Plan language for OTS transferees and the Federal Reserve System retirement program election language.",
