    "work_product": "security futures confirmations confirmation conditional disclosure analysis brief",
    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: § 240.10b-10 Confirmation of transactions.]\n\n(i) The date the transaction was executed, the identity of the single security or narrow-based security index underlying the contract for the security futures product, the number of contracts of such security futures product purchased or sold, the price, and the delivery month;\n\n(ii) The source and amount of any remuneration received or to be received by the broker or dealer in connection with the transaction, including, but not limited to, markups, commissions, costs, fees, and other charges incurred in connection with the transaction, provided, however, that if no remuneration is to be paid for an initiating transaction until the occurrence of the corresponding liquidating transaction, that the broker or dealer may disclose the amount of remuneration only on the confirmation for the liquidating transaction;\n\n(iii) The fact that information about the time of the execution of the transaction, the identity of the other party to the contract, and whether the broker or dealer is acting as agent for such customer, as agent for some other person, as agent for both such customer and some other person, or as principal for its own account, and if the broker or dealer is acting as principal, whether it is engaging in a block transaction or an exchange of security futures products for physical securities, will be available upon written request of the customer; and\n\n(iv) Whether payment for order flow is received by the broker or dealer for such transactions, the amount of this payment and the fact that the source and nature of the compensation received in connection with the particular transaction will be furnished upon written request of the customer; provided, however, that brokers or dealers that do not receive payment for order flow have no disclosure obligation under this paragraph.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a security futures confirmations conditional disclosure analysis brief for a compliance reviewer. Use only the supplied text of § 240.10b-10, Confirmation of transactions, and identify it as current Commission rule text rather than staff guidance, a proposal, or an illustrative example. Preserve the rule's exact terminology, conditions, and disclosure elements without paraphrasing or substituting terms such as 'security futures product,' 'single security or narrow-based security index,' 'remuneration,' 'liquidating transaction,' or 'payment for order flow.'\n\nStructure the brief with the following sections in this order: a purpose statement, a 'Governing Rule' section, a 'Conditional Disclosure Analysis' section, and an 'Open Items' section. Write in a neutral, precise tone suitable for a compliance reviewer who must verify each disclosure obligation against the rule text, distinguishing mandatory disclosure elements from conditional disclosure elements without editorializing.\n\nIn the 'Governing Rule' section, map each required disclosure element and each conditional trigger back to the specific clause of § 240.10b-10 from which it is derived, citing the governing standard as the source for every disclosure obligation identified.\n\nIn the 'Conditional Disclosure Analysis' section, cover the following disclosure topics separately: (1) transaction execution date and underlying security or index identity; (2) number of contracts, price, and delivery month; (3) remuneration source and amount; (4) availability of execution time, counterparty identity, and capacity information upon written request; and (5) payment for order flow disclosure. For each conditional disclosure obligation, state the precise condition under which the disclosure requirement is triggered or the disclosure obligation does not arise, using the rule's own conditional language rather than a general summary. In particular, address the rule's provision that if no remuneration is to be paid for an initiating transaction until the occurrence of the corresponding liquidating transaction, the broker or dealer may disclose the amount of remuneration only on the confirmation for the liquidating transaction, and the provision that brokers or dealers that do not receive payment for order flow have no disclosure obligation under that paragraph.\n\nLabel any disclosure element or condition that cannot be established from the supplied text as 'Not Determinable' rather than supplementing from outside sources. Do not add facts, thresholds, or requirements not present in the supplied rule text.",
