    "full_prompt": "MATERIAL 1:\n[Continues under: § 240.17a-4 Records to be preserved by certain exchange members, brokers and dealers.]\n\n(9) The records required to be made pursuant to § 240.15c3-3(d)(5) and (o) or § 240.18a-4, as applicable.\n\n(10) The records required to be made pursuant to § 240.15c3-4 and the results of the periodic reviews conducted pursuant to § 240.15c3-4(d).\n\n(11) All notices relating to an internal broker-dealer system provided to the customers of the broker or dealer that sponsors such internal broker-dealer system, as defined in paragraph (a)(16)(ii)(A) of § 240.17a-3. Notices, whether written or communicated through the internal broker-dealer trading system or other automated means, must be preserved under this paragraph (b)(11) if they are provided to all customers with access to an internal broker-dealer system, or to one or more classes of customers. Examples of notices to be preserved under this paragraph (b)(11) include, but are not limited to, notices addressing hours of system operations, system malfunctions, changes to system procedures, maintenance of hardware and software, and instructions pertaining to access to the internal broker-dealer system.\n\n(12) The records required to be made pursuant to § 240.15c3-1e(c)(4)(vi) or § 240.18a-1(e)(2)(iii)(F)( 2 ), as applicable.\n\nMATERIAL 2:\n[Continues under: § 240.17a-3 Records to be made by certain exchange members, brokers and dealers.]\n\n(A) The memorandum must show the terms and conditions of the order or instructions and of any modification or cancellation thereof, the account for which entered, the time the order was received, the time of entry, the price at which executed, the identity of each associated person, if any, responsible for the account, the identity of any other person who entered or accepted the order on behalf of the customer, or, if a customer entered the order on an electronic system, a notation of that entry; and, to the extent feasible, the time of execution or cancellation. The memorandum need not show the identity of any person, other than the associated person responsible for the account, who may have entered or accepted the order if the order is entered into an electronic system that generates the memorandum and if that system is not capable of receiving an entry of the identity of any person other than the responsible associated person; in that circumstance, the member, broker or dealer must produce upon request by a representative of a securities regulatory authority a separate record which identifies each other person. An order entered pursuant to the exercise of discretionary authority by the member, broker or dealer, or associated person thereof, must be so designated. The term instruction must include instructions between partners and employees of a member, broker or dealer. The term time of entry means the time when the member, broker or dealer transmits the order or instruction for execution.\n\nQUERY:\nPrepare a recordkeeping implementation evidence specification brief for the preservation of internal broker-dealer system notices under § 240.17a-4(b)(11), using only the two supplied rule excerpts from § 240.17a-4 and § 240.17a-3. Begin the brief with a one-sentence statement of the controlling preservation duty for internal broker-dealer system notices as set out in § 240.17a-4(b)(11).\n\nTreat both excerpts as rule text: do not present the examples of notices in § 240.17a-4(b)(11) — hours of system operations, system malfunctions, changes to system procedures, maintenance of hardware and software, and instructions pertaining to access — as an exhaustive or mandatory list, and do not add any external requirement or assumption. Where a needed implementation evidence element cannot be established from the two supplied provisions, label it `Not Determinable` rather than estimating it.\n\nPresent the specification as a table with the following columns: `Preservation Provision`, `Record-Making Condition`, `Required Implementation Evidence`, and `Source Citation`. For every row, identify the exact paragraph and sentence from § 240.17a-4(b)(11) or § 240.17a-3 that imposes the requirement, and preserve the exact cross-references, paragraph numbers, defined terms, and conditional language used in the source text.\n\nIn the table, address at least the following cross-provision elements:\n\n1. The preservation duty in § 240.17a-4(b)(11) for all notices relating to an internal broker-dealer system provided to customers of the sponsoring broker or dealer, as defined in paragraph (a)(16)(ii)(A) of § 240.17a-3, and the scope condition that notices must be preserved if they are provided to all customers with access to an internal broker-dealer system, or to one or more classes of customers.\n2. The record-making condition in § 240.17a-3 for an order memorandum entered on an electronic system, including the conditional trigger that the memorandum need not show the identity of any person other than the responsible associated person if the order is entered into an electronic system that generates the memorandum and if that system is not capable of receiving an entry of the identity of any person other than the responsible associated person. If that condition is present, require as a preservation evidence element the separate record identifying each other person that the member, broker or dealer must produce upon request by a representative of a securities regulatory authority.\n3. The discretionary-authority designation condition in § 240.17a-3: an order entered pursuant to the exercise of discretionary authority by the member, broker or dealer, or associated person thereof, must be so designated. Identify this designation as an approval or authorization condition that must be evidenced before the related order memorandum can satisfy the preservation chain, and preserve any missing designation as an open condition.\n4. The preservation control design for internal broker-dealer system notices, including the scope of notices to be retained, the classes of customers to whom notices are provided, and the operational evidence needed to demonstrate compliance with § 240.17a-4(b)(11).\n\nFor each element, connect the active evidence, the applicable rule text, and the resulting implementation evidence requirement in one traceable chain; do not present the preservation duty, the record-making conditions, and the required evidence as disconnected fragments. Close the brief with a `Next action:` line identifying the highest-priority unresolved evidence gap among the elements addressed.",
